import type { Difficulty } from "@prisma/client";

/**
 * Second content volume, authored as CSV so it can be reviewed, diffed and
 * extended without touching TypeScript.
 *
 * Difficulty is a deliberate mix inside every file rather than one value per
 * file, because the bank seeder distributes questions round-robin across
 * levels: a single-difficulty file would skew the whole grid.
 */

export type CsvQuestion = {
  topic: string;
  text: string;
  options: string[];
  correct: number;
  explanation: string;
  difficulty: Difficulty;
  tags?: string;
};

/** Raw CSV blocks, one file per topic. */
const FILES: Record<string, string> = {};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FILES["aws"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
aws,"Which AWS service gives you a resizable block storage volume that attaches to a single EC2 instance?","[""EBS"", ""EFS"", ""FSx for Lustre"", ""S3"""]",0,"EBS is a block device that attaches to one instance at a time (or several with io2 Multi-Attach). EFS is a shared NFS file system, FSx is Lustre, and S3 is object storage reached over HTTP rather than as a mounted volume.",EASY,storage
aws,"What does an S3 bucket policy Resource element with a wildcard on the object name allow?","[""Read any object in the bucket"", ""Write any object in the bucket"", ""All S3 actions on those objects"", ""Only public objects"""]",2,"A statement grants the listed actions on the listed resources. Resource ""arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*"" means every object, and s3:* means every S3 action on those objects. This is why a policy with both ""*"" action and ""*"" resource is equivalent to making the bucket public.",EASY,storage
aws,"Which S3 storage class is designed for data accessed roughly once per quarter, at the lowest per-GB price?","[""S3 Standard-IA"", ""S3 Intelligent-Tiering"", ""S3 Glacier Deep Archive"", ""S3 One Zone-IA"""]",2,"Glacier Deep Archive has the lowest per-gigabyte cost with a 180-day minimum storage duration and 12-hour retrieval. If access is once a quarter, Glacier Flexible Retrieval (minutes to hours, 90-day minimum) is usually the better fit. Standard-IA is for frequent access.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"An EC2 instance in a private subnet has no NAT gateway in its route table. What happens when it tries to reach the internet?","[""It reaches the internet over the internet gateway"", ""It cannot reach the internet; outbound traffic has no route"", ""It reaches the internet over IPv6 only"", ""It is blocked by the instance security group"""]",1,"Reaching the internet requires an explicit route to either the internet gateway (public subnets) or a NAT gateway (private subnets). With neither, TCP connections hang until timeout. A VPC endpoint is the alternative for S3 and DynamoDB specifically.",EASY,vpc
aws,"Why is an S3 Gateway VPC endpoint's bucket policy often required in addition to the endpoint route?","[""Because the endpoint needs public internet access"", ""Because routing to the endpoint is not enough; the bucket policy must also allow it"", ""Because S3 buckets always need a policy"", ""Because the endpoint replaces the security group"""]",1,"Routing alone does not grant access. S3 evaluates the request against the bucket policy, and it does not know the request arrived via the endpoint. Without allowing the endpoint's prefix list or aws:SourceVpce in the policy, requests are denied even though they never leave the VPC.",HARD,vpc
aws,"What is the difference between Multi-AZ and a read replica in RDS?","[""Multi-AZ is a synchronous standby for failover; a read replica is an asynchronous and readable"", ""Multi-AZ is for read scaling"", ""Read replicas are synchronous and Multi-AZ is asynchronous"", ""They are two names for the same feature"""]",0,"Multi-AZ maintains a synchronous standby in another AZ that fails over automatically, but you cannot read from it. A read replica is asynchronous, readable, and must be promoted manually, which introduces replication lag and potential data loss at promotion.",MEDIUM,rds
aws,"Why does raising max_connections on an RDS instance make a connection-exhaustion incident worse?","[""Each connection consumes memory, so more permitted connections exhaust server memory faster"", ""It disables connection pooling"", ""It has no effect"", ""It only affects read replicas"""]",0,"The limit is a ceiling, not a target. Raising it lets more concurrent connections consume memory until the instance runs out. The real fix is pooling with RDS Proxy or an application-side pool sized from the database limit.",MEDIUM,rds
aws,"What is the purpose of a DynamoDB global secondary index?","[""To query data using attributes other than the table's partition or sort key"", ""To replicate the table across regions"", ""To enforce referential integrity"", ""To store binary attributes more efficiently"""]",0,"A GSI lets you query on any attribute combination, at the cost of duplicating data into the index and eventual consistency: GSI reads cannot be strongly consistent. A local secondary index is the alternative when you want the same partition key with a different sort key.",MEDIUM,dynamodb
aws,"Which SQS behaviour matters most when designing an idempotent consumer?","[""Standard queues deliver at least once, so a message may be processed twice"", ""Messages are delivered in strict order"", ""A message is deleted the moment it is received"", ""FIFO queues guarantee exactly-once delivery to consumers"""]",0,"At-least-once delivery means the same message can arrive more than once, so handlers must be idempotent. FIFO ordering and deduplication exist but deduplication is window-based, not a true exactly-once guarantee for side effects.",MEDIUM,sqs
aws,"An ALB returns 503 for all requests while health checks report all targets healthy. What is the most likely cause?","[""A target group rule is misrouting, or the listener rule points at an empty target group"", ""The clients cannot resolve DNS"", ""The certificate has expired"", ""The ALB security group blocks port 443"""]",0,"503 from an ALB means no healthy targets were available to serve the request. If health checks pass, the targets are healthy, so the request is likely hitting a different or empty target group via a listener rule. This differs from 502, where the target was reachable but the exchange failed.",MEDIUM,alb
aws,"What does the CrossZoneLoadBalancingEnabled attribute on an Application Load Balancer do?","[""Routes traffic to targets in all enabled AZs even if the target's AZ is unhealthy or has no targets"", ""Moves traffic to another region"", ""Enables sticky sessions"", ""Distributes traffic evenly by hash"""]",0,"With cross-zone load balancing on, the ALB does not confine traffic to targets in the requesting AZ. That matters with an uneven target count across AZs, where the AZ with fewer targets would otherwise be overloaded. NLB has this off by default for exactly that reason.",MEDIUM,alb
aws,"Why do S3 cross-region backups have a minimum storage duration?","[""To amortise the per-GB charge across an expected minimum retention period"", ""Because objects cannot be deleted early"", ""Because retrieval takes 12 hours"", ""Because of the free tier"""]",0,"Glacier classes charge a small per-GB fee on top of low storage cost to cover the risk of short retention. Deleting early still incurs the remaining minimum-duration charge. This is a cost design decision, not a technical constraint.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"Which header should you use to serve a private S3 object to a signed URL consumer without exposing the bucket?","[""A bucket policy that allows only the signed requestor"", ""A presigned GET URL with a short expiry"", ""Set the object ACL to public-read"", ""Enable Block Public Access"""]",1,"A presigned URL carries a signature and an expiry in the request itself, so the bucket can remain private and Block Public Access can stay on. Bucket policies and ACLs are not the right tool for per-request time-limited access.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"What does an IAM role trust policy's Principal element identify?","[""Which principals are allowed to assume the role"", ""Which services the role can access"", ""Which actions the role can perform"", ""Which resources the role owns"""]",0,"The trust policy answers ""who may assume this role"", the permissions policy answers ""what the role may do once assumed"". A trust policy naming an account alone still requires sts:AssumeRole permission on the caller's side.",EASY,iam
aws,"When an EC2 instance uses an IAM role instead of access keys, what happens to its credentials after a stop and start?","[""They are re-fetched from the metadata service on next use"", ""They persist on the instance disk"", ""They expire permanently"", ""They are rotated only every 12 hours"""]",0,"Credentials are never written to disk. The instance profile delivers short-lived credentials on demand from the IMDS endpoint, so a restart transparently recovers access. This is why a restart fixes long-lived AccessDenied errors.",MEDIUM,iam
aws,"Which S3 feature best supports a compliance requirement to prove an object was not altered?","[""Bucket versioning"", ""S3 Object Lock in compliance mode"", ""S3 Transfer Acceleration"", ""Cross-Region Replication"""]",1,"Object Lock in compliance mode makes objects immutable for a retention period and cannot be shortened or deleted by anyone, including the root user. Versioning alone still allows a delete marker to hide the object.",HARD,storage
aws,"What is the effect of a VPC peering connection's default route behaviour between three VPCs A, B, C where A peers with B and B peers with C?","[""A cannot reach C; peering is not transitive"", ""A can reach C using B as a transit hop"", ""B must enable a transit gateway for A to reach C"", ""Route propagation makes it automatic"""]",0,"Routing information does not flow transitively through a peering connection. To connect all three you need a mesh of three peerings, or a Transit Gateway. This is a standard interview question and a common source of surprise outages.",MEDIUM,vpc
aws,"You need 5 TB of data copied between AWS Regions once, then deleted from the source. Which approach is cheapest?","[""S3 Cross-Region Replication with a lifecycle expiration"", ""Two Snowball devices"", ""A VPC peering connection and rsync"", ""AWS DataSync over a Direct Connect link"""]",0,"For a one-time bulk copy between S3 buckets, CRR configured once with a lifecycle rule that expires the copy is the lowest cost at this scale. Snowball becomes competitive at tens of terabytes; Direct Connect is for sustained high-volume transfer.",HARD,storage
aws,"What is a key difference between an Application Load Balancer and a Network Load Balancer?","[""ALB understands HTTP and can route by path or header; NLB forwards by port at layer 4"", ""NLB can route by URL path"", ""ALB operates at layer 4"", ""NLB terminates TLS only"""]",0,"ALB parses HTTP, so it can route by path, host or header, terminate TLS and support WebSockets and sticky sessions. NLB forwards packets by port, preserves the client IP, and handles very high throughput with lower latency.",MEDIUM,alb
aws,"Which service would you use for real-time sub-second search over billions of streaming events with no capacity planning?","[""Amazon OpenSearch Service"", ""Amazon Athena"", ""Amazon RDS"", ""Amazon ElastiCache"""]",0,"OpenSearch is a managed search and analytics engine that scales nodes automatically and gives sub-second responses over streaming data. Athena is cheap for ad-hoc SQL over a data lake but is not built for high-QPS dashboards.",MEDIUM,analytics
aws,"What does choosing a gp3 volume over gp2 mainly give you?","[""Independently scalable IOPS and throughput, with performance included in the baseline"", ""Lower baseline IOPS"", ""Exclusive access to the host"", ""Automatic replication across AZs"""]",0,"gp3 separates performance from capacity: 3000 IOPS and 125 MB/s are included, and you scale either dimension independently up to 16000 IOPS and 1000 MB/s. gp2 bundled burstable performance with capacity, which made steady workloads unpredictable.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"Why is a NAT gateway in a single AZ a single point of failure for outbound traffic?","[""It is zonal, so an AZ failure removes all outbound internet access"", ""It scales automatically across AZs"", ""It is region-scoped"", ""It only fails under high load"""]",0,"A NAT gateway exists in exactly one AZ. If that AZ becomes unavailable, instances relying on it lose outbound connectivity. The recommended pattern is one NAT gateway per AZ with the AZ-independent route pointing at the internet gateway, keeping the path within a single AZ.",MEDIUM,vpc
aws,"Which statement about S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is accurate?","[""Objects become available in minutes to hours, with a 90-day minimum storage duration"", ""Objects are available in milliseconds"", ""There is no minimum storage duration"", ""Objects are available after 12 hours with no retrieval cost"""]",0,"Flexible Retrieval balances cost against access time: minutes to hours, 90-day minimum, plus a per-GB retrieval charge. Instant Retrieval is the millisecond option with a 30-day minimum, and Deep Archive is 12 hours with 180 days.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"What is the safest first step when an ECS Fargate task is failing health checks right after a deploy?","[""Check the task's stopped-reason and inspect the logs in CloudWatch for that stopped task"", ""Increase the container memory limit"", ""Replace the task definition with the previous version and delete the service"", ""Restart every task definition in the cluster"""]",0,"The stopped task's logs survive the replacement, so they usually state the failure directly. Scaling memory before reading the error is guesswork, and deleting the service is disproportionate when a rollback or a single field change would fix it.",MEDIUM,ecs
aws,"Which IAM construct can cap what a principal can ever do regardless of any identity-based policy it receives?","[""An SCP on the account or OU"", ""A resource-based policy"", ""An inline policy"", ""A session policy that grants more permissions"""]",0,"An SCP applies to every principal in the account or OU and acts as a ceiling. Identity-based and resource-based policies can only narrow it further, because an explicit deny always wins. A session policy can restrict but not exceed what the principal already has.",MEDIUM,iam
aws,"What does Route 53 latency-based routing use to choose a record?","[""The latency measured by AWS health checks from each region"", ""The geographic location of the client as seen by DNS"", ""Round-robin weighting"", ""The record with the lowest TTL"""]",0,"Latency-based routing uses AWS health checks to pick the record with the lowest measured latency for the requesting region. Geolocation routing is separate and uses the resolver's view of the client location, which is less precise.",MEDIUM,dns
aws,"In the AWS shared responsibility model, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system of an EC2 instance?","[""The customer"", ""AWS"", ""Both, split by layer"", ""The AMI provider"""]",1,"AWS patches the host hypervisor and the physical infrastructure. Once the guest OS runs on your instance, OS patching, hardening and configuration are the customer's responsibility, as is the application on top of that.",EASY,security
aws,"What is the effect of setting an S3 bucket's MFA Delete on the versioning configuration?","[""Deletes require an MFA code and cannot be undone"", ""Deletes require MFA but versioning is disabled"", ""Only the bucket owner can delete"", ""Deletes are blocked entirely"""]",0,"MFA Delete adds a second authentication factor to permanently removing a version. It protects against an attacker who obtains the bucket credentials but not the MFA device, at the cost of making permanent deletion harder for you too.",HARD,storage
aws,"Which configuration makes an SQS queue's visibility timeout behave correctly with a 30-second Lambda function?","[""Set the visibility timeout to at least six times the function timeout"", ""Set it to 30 seconds"", ""Set it to the longest possible value"", ""Disable the visibility timeout"""]",0,"AWS guidance is that the visibility timeout should be at least six times the function timeout, plus the retry backoff window. Setting it equal to the function timeout causes another instance to pick up the message while the first is still processing, leading to duplicate side effects.",HARD,sqs
aws,"What does an AWS Auto Scaling group's health check grace period control?","[""How long after launch before health checks determine instance health"", ""How long the instance waits before joining the load balancer"", ""The cooldown between scaling activities"", ""The maximum time an instance can stay unhealthy"""]",0,"The grace period is the delay between an instance launching and health checks beginning to count against it, so slow-starting instances are not killed prematurely. The load balancer's deregistration delay handles the draining problem, which is a different mechanism.",MEDIUM,autoscaling
aws,"Which service provides a managed message broker for event-driven integration with an order guarantee and transactional consumption?","[""Amazon MQ"", ""Amazon SNS"", ""Amazon EventBridge"", ""Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"""]",0,"Amazon MQ is managed RabbitMQ or ActiveMQ, giving you queue semantics with acknowledgements and transactions. SNS is pub/sub fan-out with no consumer offset, EventBridge is event routing, and Firehose is delivery into storage, not consumption.",MEDIUM,messaging
aws,"What is the correct way to make a Lambda function read a secret that must not appear in environment variables?","[""Use the AWS SDK to fetch it from Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store at runtime"", ""Encrypt it in the environment variable"", ""Base64 encode it in the environment variable"", ""Store it in the function's description"""]",0,"Fetch at runtime from a secret store and grant the execution role read access on that specific secret. Encryption or encoding does not help, because anything in the function configuration is visible to anyone who can read it, and it appears in logs if printed.",MEDIUM,serverless
aws,"When would you choose S3 Standard-IA rather than S3 Standard for data with irregular access?","[""When objects are larger than 128 KB and accessed less than once a month"", ""For files under 10 KB accessed constantly"", ""For data that must be retrieved in milliseconds every second"", ""Only for objects in another region"""]",0,"Standard-IA has a 128 KB minimum billable size and a 30-day minimum duration, so small frequently-accessed objects cost more. It suits larger objects with infrequent access, such as backups or data warehouse staging files.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"What does AWS Trusted Advisor's high-availability checks assess for EC2?","[""Whether instances are in multiple AZs with Elastic IPs or load balancer attached"", ""Whether instances use gp3 volumes"", ""Whether IAM roles are used"", ""Whether instances are running the latest AMI"""]",0,"The EC2 HA check flags instances running in a single AZ without an Elastic IP, which makes them a single point of failure for anything that needs stable addressing. It is one of the checks covered on the paid support plans.",MEDIUM,ec2
aws,"Which statement about RDS Proxy is accurate?","[""It multiplexes many application connections onto fewer database connections and speeds up failover"", ""It caches query results to reduce load"", ""It replicates the database to another region"", ""It replaces the need for a connection pool in application code"""]",0,"RDS Proxy pools and multiplexes connections and hides the brief failover window. It does not cache results and it does not replicate. You can reduce or remove application-side pooling, though keeping a small pool is usually still right for connection churn.",MEDIUM,rds
aws,"What is required for an S3 bucket to be accessible from an EC2 instance in another AWS account via a VPC endpoint?","[""A bucket policy allowing the endpoint's prefix list, plus a route in the calling account's VPC"", ""Only a VPC endpoint in the caller's VPC"", ""Only a public-read ACL"", ""An internet gateway in the caller's VPC"""]",0,"Both sides need to agree: the caller's route table needs the endpoint route, and the bucket policy must allow the endpoint's prefix list or aws:SourceVpce. With cross-account access you also need the bucket owner to grant access, since the caller's own credentials are not the resource owner.",HARD,vpc
aws,"What does an EBS gp3 volume's throughput of 125 MB/s represent?","[""The included baseline bandwidth that can be scaled independently of IOPS"", ""The IOPS limit"", ""The maximum IOPS"", ""The burst limit shared with gp2"""]",0,"Throughput is bandwidth in MB/s and IOPS is operations per second; gp3 lets you scale them independently. 125 MB/s is included with the baseline 3000 IOPS, and you pay to raise either dimension beyond that.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"For a stateful workload needing 20 TB of throughput storage shared across dozens of EC2 instances with POSIX semantics, which service fits best?","[""FSx for Lustre"", ""EBS with Multi-Attach"", ""S3 with the Mountpoint client"", ""EFS"""]",0,"FSx for Lustre is designed for high-throughput shared POSIX storage for HPC and ML workloads. EFS is shared NFS and scales fine for general use but not to Lustre throughput. S3 is object storage, so even with Mountpoint the semantics differ.",HARD,storage
aws,"Which action is required to make an AWS account root account usable?","[""Enable multi-factor authentication"", ""Create an IAM administrator user"", ""Enable CloudTrail"", ""Set a budget"""]",0,"MFA on the root account is the single most important step, because root credentials are the ultimate backstop and are not protected by IAM policies. The rest are strongly recommended but root is the one that cannot be recovered without.",EASY,security
aws,"What does an Elastic IP address do when attached to a stopped EC2 instance?","[""It remains associated but cannot serve traffic, and you are charged for the unused address"", ""It is released automatically"", ""It is reassigned to another instance"", ""It continues to route traffic to the last instance"""]",0,"An Elastic IP stays associated with a stopped instance and does not forward traffic, but you keep paying unless it is released. AWS also charges for public IPv4 addresses generally, so release unused ones and prefer a load balancer for anything needing stable ingress.",MEDIUM,ec2
aws,"Which feature lets you keep access to an S3 bucket if the AWS account that owns it is deleted?","[""Nothing; bucket ownership is tied to the account"", ""Cross-region replication to a bucket in another account"", ""S3 Versioning"", ""An S3 Access Point in a different account"""]",1,"Replication to a bucket owned by a different account is the only pattern that survives deletion of the owning account. This is why object lock and replication to a separate account are both recommended for compliance workloads, despite the extra cost.",HARD,storage
aws,"What is the effect of setting an instance's tenancy to dedicated on its cost and placement?","[""It runs on dedicated hardware and costs more, and must be in an AZ with dedicated capacity"", ""It shares hardware but guarantees no neighbours"", ""It has no effect on placement or cost"", ""It is only available in Local Zones"""]",0,"Dedicated tenancy places the instance on hardware not shared with other AWS accounts, at a higher cost, and requires dedicated capacity availability in the AZ. It addresses a compliance requirement about physical isolation rather than performance.",HARD,ec2
aws,"Which check should you run first to determine whether a high-CPU alert is real rather than a scraping artefact?","[""Compare the CPU metric against the container's actual throttling and the node's utilisation"", ""Restart the instance"", ""Increase the Auto Scaling group's desired count"", ""Delete and recreate the alarm"""]",0,"CPU credits exhaustion and container throttling can make CPU utilisation look alarming while throughput is fine. Comparing the metric with throttle counts and node-level data tells you whether there is a real problem before you take action that makes things worse.",MEDIUM,monitoring
aws,"In AWS, what does a service control policy's explicit Deny on an action do when an identity-based policy allows the same action?","[""The request is denied; explicit deny always wins"", ""The identity-based policy wins"", ""It depends on which policy was attached last"", ""The action is allowed with a warning"""]",0,"Evaluation is deny-first across all applicable policies: identity, resource, SCP and permissions boundary. An explicit Deny anywhere cannot be overridden by an Allow, which is what makes SCPs and boundaries reliable guardrails.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"What is the correct way to let a Lambda function triggered by S3 process many objects without exhausting concurrency?","[""Use an SQS queue as a buffer with reserved concurrency on the consumer function"", ""Increase the S3 bucket's versioning"", ""Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration"", ""Attach the function to a VPC endpoint"""]",0,"S3 notifications fan out faster than Lambda concurrency allows, so events are dropped. SQS between them buffers the backlog. Reserved concurrency caps the consumer so it protects its downstream dependency rather than exhausting the shared pool.",MEDIUM,serverless
aws,"Which two AWS services share the same underlying key-value access pattern but differ in consistency guarantees?","[""DynamoDB and ElastiCache for Redis"", ""S3 and EBS"", ""RDS and Redshift"", ""Route 53 and CloudFront"""]",0,"Both are low-latency key-value stores. DynamoDB gives durable, strongly or eventually consistent reads with managed scaling; Redis is an in-memory cache where data is typically not treated as authoritative. Confusing them in a design review is a common source of cache-coherency bugs.",EASY,architecture
aws,"What is the purpose of an AWS Outposts availability zone?","[""An on-premises location that extends an AWS region's API and networking into a customer facility"", ""A private 5G network for IoT devices"", ""A physically isolated region for classified workloads"", ""An edge cache location"""]",0,"An Outpost brings a full rack of AWS hardware into your data centre so on-premises latency feels like in-region latency, and connects to the region over Direct Connect or a site-to-site VPN. It is not a separate region and does not provide data residency on its own.",HARD,architecture
aws,"Which S3 storage class offers millisecond retrieval, a 30-day minimum storage duration, and a 128 KB minimum billable size?","[""S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval"", ""S3 Standard-IA"", ""S3 One Zone-Standard"", ""S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval"""]",0,"Instant Retrieval keeps objects in low-cost storage while retaining millisecond access, so it fits data with a 30-day-plus access pattern that cannot tolerate retrieval delays. The 128 KB minimum billable size means it is a poor choice for many small objects.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"What does AWS KMS grant in the context of an S3 SSE-KMS bucket?","[""A unique data key per object, with the key policy controlling who can decrypt"", ""A shared key that all bucket users can decrypt"", ""Encryption in transit only"", ""Automatic key rotation without any cost"""]",0,"SSE-KMS requests a unique data key for each object, and the key's policy decides who may decrypt. That is the difference from SSE-S3, where anyone with bucket permissions effectively has access to the plaintext. KMS calls are also billed per request, which matters for high-object-count workloads.",HARD,security
aws,"Which AWS service would you use to analyse billions of objects in S3 with SQL on demand and pay only for the bytes scanned?","[""Amazon Athena"", ""Amazon Redshift"", ""Amazon RDS"", ""Amazon QuickSight Enterprise"""]",0,"Athena queries data in place with no warehouse to load, charged per TB scanned. Redshift needs the data loaded and provisioned up front, which is wrong for exploratory one-off analysis over a large, irregular data lake.",EASY,analytics
aws,"What is the most reliable way to confirm an S3 bucket has no public access?","[""Review S3 Block Public Access settings, the bucket policy, all ACLs, and the Access Analyzer findings"", ""Check whether the bucket has a public DNS name"", ""Look at whether the bucket policy has a wildcard"", ""Check CloudFront in front of the bucket"""]",0,"Public access can arrive by four independent routes: the bucket policy, an object or bucket ACL, a CloudFront OAC misconfiguration, or a policy resource typo. Block Public Access plus S3 Access Analyzer is the combination that actually proves it, which is why public access blocks are now on by default.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"Which statement about Auto Scaling scheduled actions is correct?","[""They scale proactively at set times and cost nothing to configure"", ""They react to CloudWatch alarms"", ""They replace target-tracking policies"", ""They only work with Spot Instances"""]",0,"Scheduled actions fire on a schedule, which suits known demand patterns such as a nightly batch or a business-hours ramp. They coexist with, rather than replace, reactive policies. Cost is not the concern; predictability is the reason to use them.",EASY,autoscaling
aws,"What happens to an S3 object when you change its storage class to Glacier Instant Retrieval while the bucket has an object lock retention period?","[""The lock is unaffected; retention is independent of storage class"", ""The lock is released so the class change can proceed"", ""The object cannot change class at all"", ""The retention period is reset"""]",0,"Object Lock governs deletion and version replacement, not the storage class. Storage class, replication and metadata changes are all permitted while the object is locked, which is what makes long-term archival practical under compliance.",HARD,storage
aws,"Which service gives you a private DNS zone resolvable only from inside a VPC, for naming internal services?","[""A private hosted zone in Route 53"", ""A public hosted zone with a private TTL"", ""VPC DNS resolver with a custom domain"", ""AWS PrivateLink alone"""]",0,"A private hosted zone is served by the VPC's own DNS resolver at the .2 address of the VPC CIDR, so only resolvers inside the VPC can query it. Public zones are resolvable from anywhere, which is not what an internal service name wants.",EASY,dns
aws,"In an IAM policy, what does an explicit Deny in a permissions boundary do?","[""Overrides any Allow in an identity-based policy for that principal"", ""Is overridden by an identity-based Allow"", ""Only applies to IAM groups"", ""Applies only at the account level"""]",0,"A permissions boundary intersects with identity-based policies, so a boundary Deny caps what the principal can ever do and cannot be widened by any inline or managed policy. This is the standard mechanism for constraining delegated administration.",MEDIUM,iam
aws,"What does an AWS ALB do when a target group has no registered targets and the listener rule is weighted across two groups?","[""Returns 503 only when all groups for the rule are empty; a group with targets still receives traffic"", ""Returns 502 because it cannot connect"", ""Removes the listener rule automatically"", ""Spreads traffic across all registered targets in the account"""]",0,"A 503 means the rule resolved to target groups that are all empty. If one group in the rule still has healthy targets, traffic flows to it. So a partial failure presents as uneven traffic rather than an outage, which makes it easy to miss without per-group metrics.",HARD,alb
aws,"Which change makes an S3 bucket's objects accessible from a VPC endpoint while also keeping them private?","[""Restrict the bucket policy to the endpoint's prefix list and keep Block Public Access enabled"", ""Make the objects public and rely on the endpoint for speed"", ""Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration"", ""Add a second S3 bucket in the public region"""]",0,"A prefix list condition on the endpoint plus Block Public Access gives you private, private-network access. Making objects public would work but defeats the purpose and introduces a public exposure risk.",MEDIUM,vpc
aws,"What is the main difference between AWS Backup and a manual snapshot schedule?","[""AWS Backup provides a central policy, retention and cross-region copy managed in one place"", ""AWS Backup is cheaper for single volumes"", ""Manual snapshots cannot be automated"", ""AWS Backup only supports RDS"""]",0,"AWS Backup centralises lifecycle policies, retention, cross-region copy and tagging across supported resource types, and integrates with compliance reporting. Manual snapshots are still valid for point-in-time copies, but governance is manual and inconsistent across services.",MEDIUM,backup
aws,"Which service provides a fully managed key-value store with on-demand capacity and no server to size?","[""Amazon DynamoDB"", ""Amazon RDS"", ""Amazon EBS"", ""Self-managed Memcached on EC2"""]",0,"DynamoDB is serverless in the sense that capacity scales automatically or can be set to on-demand, with no instances to size. RDS requires choosing an instance class, and a self-managed cache means you own the sizing and the failover.",EASY,dynamodb
aws,"What does an EC2 instance's placement group tenancy of cluster do?","[""Packs instances onto the same underlying hardware for low-latency, low-network-latency communication"", ""Spreads instances across AZs for failure isolation"", ""Restricts instances to one dedicated host"", ""Enables Hibernation"""]",0,"Cluster placement packs instances close together for HPC and low-latency distributed systems, trading failure-domain isolation for network performance. Spread placement is the opposite intent, and host tenancy pins to a dedicated host.",HARD,ec2
aws,"Which statement about Route 53 health checks is correct?","[""A record can be set to fail over to a secondary only when its own health check fails, using Failover records"", ""Health checks can be attached to weighted records to remove a target automatically"", ""Health checks must be configured on the ALB rather than the record"", ""Health checks are only available on private hosted zones"""]",0,"Failover records paired with health checks give active-passive DNS failover. Weighted and latency records can be combined with health checks and will stop sending traffic to an unhealthy target, but full failover between primary and secondary is what Failover records are for.",HARD,dns
aws,"What is the effect of enabling S3 Transfer Acceleration on a bucket?","[""Uploads and downloads between the client and S3 go over AWS edge locations over the AWS network"", ""It reduces the price of S3 Standard in the bucket's region"", ""It copies objects between regions automatically"", ""It enables multi-region access points"""]",0,"Transfer Acceleration routes traffic through AWS edge locations over the AWS backbone, which helps with long-distance or high-latency clients. It adds a per-GB cost and is not the same as CRR, which is about moving data between buckets in different regions.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"When does an AWS EC2 Auto Scaling group's Spot Instance interruption notice arrive, and what should be run on it?","[""Two minutes before interruption; checkpoint work and shut down cleanly"", ""Immediately at termination; nothing can be done"", ""30 seconds before termination; drain connections only"", ""There is no notice for Spot instances"""]",0,"Spot provides a two-minute interruption notice to the instance metadata, and your instance has 120 seconds to react. Handling that notice to checkpoint and shut down gracefully is what makes Spot viable for checkpointed workloads; ignoring it means losing whatever was in flight.",MEDIUM,spot
aws,"Which IAM best practice addresses the risk of long-term access keys in CI/CD pipelines?","[""Use OIDC federation with short-lived credentials from GitHub Actions or GitLab"", ""Rotate the access keys weekly"", ""Store the keys in the repository's secrets file"", ""Use one shared CI user for all pipelines"""]",0,"OIDC removes static keys entirely: the workflow exchanges a signed identity token for brief credentials. Rotation is a mitigation, not a fix, because the key still exists and can leak in the meantime. Shared CI users also destroy attribution.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"What does an AWS Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoint enable?","[""On-premises DNS queries to be forwarded to an Amazon-provided VPC resolver"", ""Inbound queries from the internet to on-premises DNS"", ""DNS-based load balancing between AWS regions"", ""Private DNS zone creation in on-premises DNS"""]",0,"An outbound endpoint lets on-premises resolvers forward queries to Route 53 Resolver in a VPC, so on-prem hosts can resolve private hosted zone names. An inbound endpoint does the reverse for the other direction.",HARD,dns
aws,"Why can an S3 bucket policy granting s3:GetObject to Principal ""*"" appear to work while the bucket stays private?","[""Block Public Access denies the request, overriding the policy's allow"", ""The policy has an invalid condition"", ""The wildcard is ignored"", ""S3 caches the policy for 24 hours"""]",0,"Block Public Access evaluates as an explicit deny that overrides the policy's allow. That is the intended pattern: write the policy you want, let BPA enforce the deny, so a future edit cannot silently expose the bucket.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"Which two settings determine an S3 bucket's static website hosting endpoint?","[""Static website hosting enabled and public-read access or a policy allowing GetObject"", ""Versioning and lifecycle rules"", ""Default encryption and Object Lock"", ""Multipart upload and Transfer Acceleration"""]",0,"Static website hosting requires both parts: the hosting configuration, and public read access for the objects, otherwise the endpoint returns 403. This combination is why S3 website endpoints are not a drop-in replacement for CloudFront when privacy matters.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"Which service gives a managed message queue with FIFO ordering and content-based deduplication?","[""Amazon SQS FIFO queues"", ""Amazon SNS standard topics"", ""Amazon EventBridge"", ""Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"""]",0,"SQS FIFO preserves ordering within a message group and deduplicates by content hash within a five-minute window. SNS and EventBridge are pub/sub and do not guarantee ordering, and Kinesis provides ordering per shard but is a stream rather than a queue.",MEDIUM,sqs
aws,"What is the recommended way to share an AWS KMS key across accounts?","[""Put the key policy and each caller's IAM policy in place so both allow the use"", ""Copy the key to the other account"", ""Make the key public and reference it by ARN"", ""Use the same account alias"""]",0,"Cross-account KMS access requires the key policy to name the principal (or account) AND the caller's identity policy to allow the kms action. Both sides must agree. Copying or aliasing does not grant access, and a key cannot be made public in the way an S3 bucket can.",HARD,security
aws,"What does AWS Config record that CloudTrail does not?","[""Configuration state and relationships over time, for compliance and drift queries"", ""API call events for every request"", ""Billing data per service"", ""Resource performance metrics"""]",0,"CloudTrail is an append-only log of API calls. Config tracks the state of resources and how they relate, which is what you query to prove a resource was encrypted throughout a period, or to find resources that are non-compliant right now.",MEDIUM,compliance
aws,"Which statement about AWS S3 bucket keys is accurate?","[""SSES3 is free, SSE-KMS is billed per request, and SSE-C uses customer-managed keys"", ""All three server-side encryption options are free"", ""SSE-KMS requires bringing your own key"", ""SSE-C is managed by AWS KMS"""]",0,"SSE-S3 uses AWS-managed keys at no charge, SSE-KMS uses AWS KMS keys with per-request charges, and SSE-C has you supply and manage the key material yourself. Per-request KMS cost is why very high object counts with SSE-KMS can surprise you on the bill.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"What is the effect of setting an EBS volume's DeleteOnTermination to true?","[""The volume is deleted when the attached instance is terminated, which loses data"", ""The volume snapshot is deleted"", ""The volume becomes encrypted"", ""The volume is converted to instance store"""]",0,"DeleteOnTermination defaults to true for EBS volumes attached at launch, so terminating an instance silently deletes its root and data volumes. Setting it false is essential for any volume whose data you care about, and it is a common data-loss cause in incident retrospectives.",EASY,storage
aws,"Which AWS service provides managed message queues that supports both RabbitMQ and ActiveMQ engines?","[""Amazon MQ"", ""Amazon SQS"", ""Amazon SNS"", ""Amazon EventBridge"""]",0,"Amazon MQ manages RabbitMQ or ActiveMQ brokers, which is the migration path for applications built on those brokers. SQS and SNS are simpler AWS-native primitives without broker protocol compatibility.",MEDIUM,messaging
aws,"What happens if an AWS Lambda function's handler throws after an event source has already been deleted?","[""The event is retried until the retry policy is exhausted, then discarded or sent to a dead-letter target"", ""The event is dropped immediately"", ""Lambda crashes permanently"", ""The event is retried forever"""]",0,"Partial batch failures aside, a persistent throw means repeated retries until the function's maximum event age or retry attempts is reached. With a dead-letter queue configured the event lands there; without one it is discarded after exhaustion, so DLQ configuration matters for anything you need to keep.",HARD,serverless
aws,"Which describes how AWS Secrets Manager differs from SSM Parameter Store SecureString?","[""Secrets Manager supports built-in rotation of credentials and full secret payloads"", ""Parameter Store supports rotation and secrets versioning"", ""Both are identical"", ""Secrets Manager only stores short strings"""]",0,"Secrets Manager is built for rotating credentials, with rotation integrations for RDS and other services and full secret versioning with recovery windows. Parameter Store SecureString is cheaper and simpler, intended mostly for configuration values, and rotation is not built in.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"In the AWS shared security responsibility model for RDS, who patches the underlying database engine version?","[""AWS for the managed service components; the customer for major-version upgrades and configuration"", ""The customer entirely"", ""AWS entirely"", ""The database vendor"""]",0,"AWS handles the host and applies minor engine patches on your schedule window, but major version upgrades require an explicit customer action. Security configuration, such as encryption, auditing and permissions, stays with the customer.",MEDIUM,rds
aws,"What is the effect of using a Secrets Manager or SSM SecureString value in an EC2 user data script?","[""It is visible in the instance metadata and process list, so it should be avoided"", ""It is automatically encrypted and safe"", ""It cannot be read by user data"", ""It is rotated on every boot"""]",0,"Fetching a secret in user data still leaves it in the instance's shell history, process arguments and often CloudWatch Logs. The better pattern is to fetch at runtime from the application using the instance role, so the secret never exists in a script or log.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"Which AWS storage option is best for a database that needs sub-millisecond IOPS and consistent low latency, and why not S3?","[""EBS io2 Block Express, because S3 is object storage reached over HTTP with much higher and variable latency"", ""S3 Standard, because it is designed for databases"", ""EFS, because NFS gives the lowest latency"", ""Instance store, because it is network-attached"""]",0,"io2 Block Express is designed for sub-millisecond latency and high durability with a 99.999% SLA. S3 is object storage over HTTP with tens of milliseconds of latency, so it cannot back a database's random write path, and EFS NFS has meaningfully higher latency than local block storage.",HARD,storage
aws,"What does an AWS Lambda function's reserved concurrency of zero mean?","[""The function cannot be invoked at all, which is a way to disable it"", ""The function has unlimited concurrency"", ""The function uses no memory"", ""The function is throttled to one concurrent execution"""]",0,"Setting reserved concurrency to zero stops the function being triggered, which is a clean kill switch during an incident. It also releases the function's share of the account concurrency pool back to other functions.",MEDIUM,serverless
aws,"Which AWS feature automatically moves S3 objects to a cheaper storage class after a set number of days?","[""An S3 lifecycle configuration with a transition rule"", ""S3 intelligent tiering, which requires no configuration"", ""S3 replication to a Glacier bucket"", ""S3 Batch Operations"""]",0,"A lifecycle rule transitions objects after N days, and this is the predictable, explicit choice. Intelligent tiering moves objects automatically with no configuration but at a monitoring fee and cannot use lifecycle rules on top, so you pick one or the other per bucket.",MEDIUM,storage
aws,"What is the effect of a Security Group referencing a Security Group from another VPC?","[""It is not permitted; a security group can only reference groups in the same VPC"", ""It is permitted across peered VPCs"", ""It is permitted only within the same AWS account"", ""It is permitted if both VPCs use the same CIDR"""]",0,"Security group referencing works within a single VPC only, which is why peer connections rely on explicit CIDR rules rather than group references. This is a common source of confusion when designing peering or Transit Gateway routing.",MEDIUM,vpc
aws,"Which statement about AWS Backup's cold storage tier for EC2 recovery points is accurate?","[""Recovery points in cold storage have lower cost but longer restore times"", ""Cold storage restores are instantaneous"", ""Cold storage cannot be used for EC2"", ""Cold storage is only for S3"""]",0,"Cold storage reduces per-point cost but adds hours to restore time, so it suits long retention such as regulatory retention. For anything you might need quickly, warm or hot tiers are the right choice.",MEDIUM,backup
aws,"In a private subnet with a NAT gateway, what happens to a response from the internet to an outbound-initiated connection?","[""The NAT gateway maps it back using its port mapping table"", ""The instance cannot receive it without a public IP"", ""The security group blocks inbound by default"", ""The route table discards it"""]",0,"The NAT gateway maintains a port mapping table: outbound requests allocate a public port on the gateway, and returning traffic is translated back to the private instance using that mapping. This is why unsolicited inbound connections to a NAT gateway never reach the instance.",MEDIUM,vpc
aws,"What is the correct fix for the S3 error ""Access Denied"" when an IAM principal has an IAM policy allowing GetObject and the bucket policy also allows it?","[""Usually a Block Public Access or explicit deny, or the principal's identity is not matching the assumed role"", ""The bucket policy must be attached to the object rather than the bucket"", ""The object must be unencrypted"", ""The request must use POST instead of GET"""]",0,"Explicit deny always wins, so an allow in both policies is not sufficient. A common specific cause is a bucket policy principal that does not match the actual assumed role ARN, which looks correct but never matches in practice.",MEDIUM,security
aws,"Which AWS service supports field-level encryption control with customer-managed keys without moving the data out of the service?","[""Server-side encryption with a customer-managed KMS key on the same service"", ""AWS KMS envelope encryption applied client-side only"", ""Client-side encryption into S3 with a self-managed key"", ""No AWS service offers this"""]",0,"Server-side encryption with a customer-managed KMS key gives field-level cryptographic control and key-policy separation while the data stays in S3, RDS or DynamoDB, without the client managing anything. Client-side encryption is a different and more operationally complex choice.",HARD,security
aws,"What does the AWS Well-Architected Framework's reliability pillar recommend for a stateless workload running across two Availability Zones?","[""Load balance across both AZs so an AZ failure does not remove all capacity"", ""Use a larger instance type in one AZ to absorb failure"", ""Keep all instances in one AZ to minimise inter-AZ latency"", ""Rely on Auto Scaling alone without health checks"""]",0,"Spreading stateless capacity across AZs behind a load balancer means one AZ failing leaves the other serving. A single-AZ deployment, however large the instances, is a single point of failure for that region regardless of instance size.",EASY,architecture
aws,"Which AWS setting determines whether an S3 bucket's access logs are written to a separate bucket, and what is the cost characteristic?","[""Bucket logging configuration; log delivery and storage are billed to the destination bucket owner"", ""Server access logging is free and unlimited"", ""It is a CloudWatch Logs subscription, billed at the same rate as logs"", ""It requires an AWS Config rule"""]",0,"S3 server access logging delivers to a destination bucket you own, and you pay for the objects stored there plus the request charges to write them. CloudTrail data events are a separate, more expensive option that captures object-level API calls instead.",HARD,storage
aws,"For an application that must maintain session affinity with an ALB while also needing path-based routing, which feature pair applies?","[""ALB with a target group stickiness setting and a listener rule"", ""NLB with source IP affinity and a CloudFront distribution"", ""ALB with a Network ACL affinity rule"", ""Route 53 latency routing with a sticky session flag"""]",0,"An ALB can do path-based routing and offers session stickiness per target group, which covers this case. NLB preserves client IP naturally but cannot route by path, so combining NLB with CloudFront moves path routing to CloudFront.",MEDIUM,alb
`;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FILES["kubernetes"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
kubernetes,"What is the purpose of a Kubernetes readiness probe?","[""Restarts the container when it fails"", ""Removes the pod from Service endpoints while keeping it running"", ""Pulls the image before starting"", ""Schedules the pod onto a node"""]",1,"Readiness controls traffic, not liveness. Failing readiness takes the pod out of Service endpoints so no new requests arrive, but the container keeps running and can recover. That is the right probe for slow warm-up or a temporarily unavailable dependency.",EASY,probes
kubernetes,"What does a liveness probe failure trigger?","[""The pod is removed from Service endpoints"", ""The container is restarted by the kubelet"", ""The pod is rescheduled to another node"", ""The Deployment rolls back"""]",1,"A failed liveness probe causes the kubelet to kill the container, which then restarts under the pod's restartPolicy. This is why liveness must only check the process itself: wiring it to a database turns a downstream outage into a crash loop.",EASY,probes
kubernetes,"Which QoS class results when a pod sets equal requests and limits for CPU and memory on every container?","[""Guaranteed"", ""Burstable"", ""BestEffort"", ""The default, which cannot be set"""]",0,"Guaranteed requires equal requests and limits for both CPU and memory on all containers. Those pods are the last evicted under node pressure. BestEffort (nothing set) is evicted first, and Burstable covers everything between.",MEDIUM,resources
kubernetes,"Why do you set resource requests rather than relying on limits?","[""Requests drive scheduling; limits are only a runtime ceiling"", ""Requests are optional and limits are mandatory"", ""Limits are used by the scheduler and requests are not"", ""They are interchangeable"""]",0,"The scheduler places pods using requests, which reserve capacity so nodes are not oversubscribed. Limits are enforced afterwards by the kubelet, and CPU over-limit behaviour is throttling rather than termination. Under-requesting CPU causes throttling and eviction even when the node looks half empty.",MEDIUM,scheduling
kubernetes,"What does the container exit code 137 usually indicate?","[""OOM kill from exceeding the memory limit"", ""A failed startup probe"", ""An invalid image tag"", ""A liveness probe failure"""]",0,"137 is 128 plus signal 9 (SIGKILL), most often the cgroup OOM killer because the container exceeded its memory limit. 143 is the graceful equivalent (SIGTERM). A failed liveness probe also produces a kill, so check the pod events and reason to distinguish them.",MEDIUM,troubleshooting
kubernetes,"When should you use a startup probe instead of a long initialDelaySeconds on liveness?","[""When the app needs a long, variable warm-up and you do not want liveness running during boot"", ""When the app never becomes ready"", ""When there is more than one container per pod"", ""When using a DaemonSet"""]",0,"A startup probe gates liveness and readiness: they do not begin until it succeeds. That lets you keep the normal, aggressive liveness settings for the lifetime of the process instead of inflating the delay and weakening the check.",MEDIUM,probes
kubernetes,"What does the Kubernetes scheduler use when placing a pod?","[""Container resource requests"", ""Container resource limits"", ""Current CPU usage"", ""The pod's priority class only"""]",0,"Scheduling is driven by requests so nodes are never oversubscribed at the CPU and memory level. This is why a pod can stay Pending with insufficient cpu even when the node's actual usage looks low: existing requests have reserved the capacity.",MEDIUM,scheduling
kubernetes,"Why can pods be evicted from a node even though the cluster has spare capacity elsewhere?","[""Eviction happens per node on memory or disk pressure, not across the cluster"", ""Eviction only happens when the cluster is full"", ""Pods are evicted during upgrades only"", ""Eviction requires a PodDisruptionBudget to be unset"""]",0,"The kubelet evicts pods on its own node when that node runs out of memory or ephemeral storage. Adding nodes elsewhere does not help that node. A pod eviction storm almost always means requests are misconfigured relative to real usage.",MEDIUM,eviction
kubernetes,"What is the function of a PodDisruptionBudget?","[""Constrains voluntary disruptions such as node drains during upgrades"", ""Limits how many pods a Deployment can create"", ""Prevents a pod from being scheduled on a specific node"", ""Enforces resource quotas per namespace"""]",0,"A PDB sets a floor on available replicas during voluntary disruption, so drains and cluster upgrades proceed without taking a service below its minimum. It does not protect against involuntary failures like a crash or a node failure.",MEDIUM,availability
kubernetes,"Which Service type exposes a service outside the VPC using a cloud provider load balancer?","[""LoadBalancer"", ""ClusterIP"", ""NodePort"", ""ExternalName"""]",0,"LoadBalancer provisions an external load balancer and exposes the Service outside the cluster. NodePort opens a port on every node IP, bypassing the cloud load balancer. ClusterIP is internal only.",EASY,services
kubernetes,"What is the benefit of using a NetworkPolicy?","[""It restricts traffic between pods by label, providing segmentation within a namespace"", ""It replaces the Kubernetes Service object"", ""It replaces security groups for all traffic"", ""It provides DNS resolution"""]",0,"A NetworkPolicy restricts pod-to-pod traffic by pod selector, namespace selector and port. Without one, all pods in a namespace can talk to all others. Note that most CNIs require the feature to be enabled explicitly.",MEDIUM,security
kubernetes,"How does a pod obtain an IP address in a typical cluster?","[""The CNI plugin on the node assigns it during pod sandbox creation"", ""The API server allocates it when the pod is created"", ""kube-proxy assigns it after the Service is created"", ""The kubelet assigns a ClusterIP"""]",0,"The kubelet asks the CNI plugin to create the pod's network sandbox, and the plugin assigns the address. In EKS the AWS VPC CNI attaches an ENI, which is why pods get routable private IPs. The pod shows as Pending until this completes.",MEDIUM,networking
kubernetes,"What does the imagePullPolicy of IfNotPresent do?","[""Pulls the image only if it is not already present on the node"", ""Always pulls the latest tag"", ""Never pulls, failing if the image is absent"", ""Pulls only in production namespaces"""]",0,"IfNotPresent uses the cached image when present, which speeds up restarts on a warm node. Because a tag like latest is only pulled once under this policy, that is why mutable tags combined with IfNotPresent can serve stale code.",MEDIUM,operations
kubernetes,"Why should ConfigMap values sourced through env vars require a pod restart to change?","[""env values are resolved once at container start, unlike mounted files"", ""env vars cannot be updated by the kubelet at all"", ""ConfigMaps mounted as env are read-only permanently"", ""The API server caches env values"""]",0,"Values referenced through env or envFrom are injected into the container's environment at start and never change. Mounted ConfigMap volumes are synced by the kubelet about once a minute and do update in place, so the two mechanisms behave differently and it matters which one your app uses.",HARD,config
kubernetes,"What happens to a pod with a CrashLoopBackOff state?","[""The container is repeatedly restarted with increasing backoff"", ""The pod is deleted and recreated immediately"", ""The pod is rescheduled to another node"", ""The container is paused indefinitely"""]",0,"CrashLoopBackOff means the container keeps exiting and the kubelet backs off between restarts, from 10 seconds up to a five-minute cap. The container's current logs are usually empty, so the error is in the previous instance's logs.",MEDIUM,troubleshooting
kubernetes,"How do you make a Deployment rollout back to the previous ReplicaSet quickly?","[""kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name>"", ""Delete the ReplicaSet manually"", ""Restart all the pods"", ""Change the image tag to empty"""]",0,"rollout undo reverts the Deployment's pod template, and Kubernetes then scales the previous ReplicaSet back up. Deleting the ReplicaSet leaves the Deployment to create fresh pods, which is not a rollback and loses the known-good spec.",EASY,deployments
kubernetes,"What is the effect of setting a Deployment's strategy to Recreate?","[""All existing pods are killed before new ones start, causing downtime"", ""New pods start before old ones are removed, with no downtime"", ""Only one pod is replaced at a time"", ""The Deployment is paused"""]",0,"Recreate scales the old ReplicaSet to zero before scaling the new one up, which is a deliberate choice for workloads that cannot run two versions at once, such as those with exclusive resource claims. The default RollingUpdate avoids downtime.",MEDIUM,deployments
kubernetes,"Why is a StatefulSet used instead of a Deployment for a database with persistent storage?","[""It gives each pod a stable identity and its own persistent volume claim"", ""It restarts pods faster"", ""It supports more replicas"", ""It avoids needing a Service"""]",0,"A StatefulSet provides stable network identities and per-pod persistent volume claims that follow the pod across rescheduling, which is what a database needs. Deployments give interchangeable pods, which is wrong for stateful workloads.",MEDIUM,statefulsets
kubernetes,"What does the Kubernetes scheduler's taint and toleration mechanism do?","[""Taints repel pods that do not tolerate them, reserving nodes for specific workloads"", ""Taints label pods for the scheduler to group"", ""Tolerations set resource limits on a pod"", ""Taints restrict which namespaces a node accepts pods from"""]",0,"A taint on a node repels pods lacking a matching toleration, keeping general workloads off specialised nodes such as GPU or control-plane nodes. Tolerations allow specific pods in; they do not grant scheduling by themselves, since the scheduler still needs to fit the pod.",MEDIUM,scheduling
kubernetes,"What is the purpose of a ResourceQuota in a namespace?","[""It caps the total compute and object resources a namespace can consume"", ""It assigns pods to specific nodes"", ""It enforces image pull secrets"", ""It limits the number of replicas per Deployment"""]",0,"A ResourceQuota aggregates limits across a namespace, for example CPU, memory and object counts, which prevents one team from consuming the cluster. A LimitRange sets the defaults and per-object bounds for individual pods and containers.",MEDIUM,resources
kubernetes,"Which probe configuration prevents traffic to an application that depends on a slow-starting cache?","[""A readiness probe with an appropriate initialDelaySeconds, and no liveness probe tied to the cache"", ""A liveness probe pointed at the cache"", ""No probes at all"", ""A startup probe with failureThreshold 1"""]",0,"Readiness alone is correct: the pod stays out of endpoints until the cache warms, then joins. Tying liveness to the cache is the classic mistake that converts a slow cache into a container restart loop.",MEDIUM,probes
kubernetes,"What does setting a container's request equal to its limit achieve for CPU?","[""Guaranteed CPU shares under contention, with throttling once the quota is exceeded"", ""Unlimited CPU for that container"", ""Guaranteed CPU with no throttling possible"", ""Automatic CPU burst credit"""]",0,"An equal request and limit places the pod in the Guaranteed QoS class, so under node contention it gets reserved shares. Over the limit, the cgroup throttles the container, so latency rises even though the container keeps running. Burst credits only exist on burstable instances like T2.",HARD,resources
kubernetes,"Which command reveals why a pod could not be scheduled?","[""kubectl describe pod <name>, which lists scheduling events"", ""kubectl get pods -o wide"", ""kubectl logs <name>"", ""kubectl top pod <name>"""]",0,"describe includes the Events section, which carries messages such as insufficient cpu or untolerated taint with the specific numbers. get and logs will not show scheduling failures, and top only reports running workloads.",EASY,troubleshooting
kubernetes,"What happens when a pod references a ConfigMap key that does not exist, without the optional flag?","[""The pod stays in CreateContainerConfigError"", ""The pod starts with an empty value"", ""The kubelet creates an empty ConfigMap"", ""The pod is deleted and retried forever"""]",0,"Without optional: true, the kubelet cannot build the container's config and reports CreateContainerConfigError, retrying until the key appears. With optional: true the pod starts and the value is simply absent, which is the safer choice for non-critical settings.",MEDIUM,config
kubernetes,"How does the HPA compute desired replicas from a CPU utilisation target?","[""It scales the ratio of current utilisation to the target, applied to current replicas"", ""It adds one pod whenever utilisation exceeds the target"", ""It uses memory limits instead of CPU requests"", ""It doubles replicas when utilisation doubles"""]",0,"The formula is desired = ceil(currentReplicas x currentUtilisation / targetUtilisation), averaged across pods and clamped by min and max. Crucially utilisation is measured against each pod's CPU request, so wrong requests make the HPA misbehave.",HARD,autoscaling
kubernetes,"What is the difference between a Deployment and a ReplicaSet?","[""A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to provide declarative updates and rollbacks"", ""They are identical"", ""A ReplicaSet manages Deployments"", ""A Deployment cannot update pods"""]",0,"The ReplicaSet keeps a number of pods running. The Deployment sits above it and manages ReplicaSets, which is what gives you declarative updates, controlled rollout and rollback. You rarely write ReplicaSets by hand.",EASY,deployments
kubernetes,"What does a Pod's phase of Unknown indicate?","[""The node's kubelet stopped reporting, so the control plane cannot determine pod state"", ""The pod image failed to pull"", ""The pod is being evicted"", ""The container exited non-zero"""]",0,"Unknown means communication with the kubelet was lost, which points at the node or the network path rather than the application. Check node status and the kubelet, not the workload.",MEDIUM,troubleshooting
kubernetes,"Which statement about Secrets in Kubernetes is accurate?","[""They are base64-encoded, not encrypted, unless you configure at-rest encryption"", ""They are encrypted at rest by default"", ""They are only readable by the namespace admin"", ""They are stored in etcd unencrypted but protected by RBAC alone"""]",0,"Secret data is base64-encoded, which is encoding rather than encryption. At-rest encryption needs an EncryptionConfiguration or an external KMS provider, and scoping access is down to RBAC, so treat Secret RBAC as the primary control.",MEDIUM,security
kubernetes,"What is the effect of removing a node selector from a running Deployment without changing the image?","[""Nothing happens until pods are recreated, then new pods may schedule elsewhere"", ""All pods immediately reschedule"", ""The Deployment is rolled back"", ""The node is drained"""]",0,"Pods are not rescheduled just because a template field changed. Existing pods keep running until something replaces them, at which point the new spec applies and they may land on different nodes. That delay surprises people expecting a change to take effect immediately.",HARD,scheduling
kubernetes,"When is an initContainer required rather than a startup script?","[""When setup must complete successfully before the main containers start, or when setup needs different resources or security context"", ""When the app needs environment variables"", ""When running multiple app containers"", ""When the pod needs a Service"""]",0,"An initContainer runs to completion in order before the main containers start, and a failure blocks the pod. This is the right pattern for migrations or fetching secrets, and it also allows setup to use a different image, resources or security context than the app.",MEDIUM,operations
kubernetes,"What does setting a Service's sessionAffinity to ClientIP require to work effectively?","[""The client IP must be preserved end to end, which an external load balancer may not do"", ""The Service must be headless"", ""The pods must run on one node"", ""The Service must use a NodePort"""]",0,"ClientIP affinity keys on the source IP, so it only works if that IP survives the whole path. A cloud load balancer that terminates and re-originates can change it, making affinity unreliable unless the LB preserves the address.",HARD,services
kubernetes,"What is the effect of a Pod having activeDeadlineSeconds?","[""The kubelet terminates it after the deadline and the controller replaces it"", ""It becomes read-only after the deadline"", ""The pod is paused"", ""It limits how long the container may run in total across retries"""]",0,"activeDeadlineSeconds is a hard limit on the pod's total lifetime, including retries, enforced with a Terminating state and SIGTERM followed by SIGKILL. It is a safety net for jobs that can hang forever, not a graceful timeout.",MEDIUM,operations
kubernetes,"Which pattern reduces a Deployment's blast radius without changing the code?","[""Spread replicas across nodes with topologySpreadConstraints or podAntiAffinity"", ""Increase the replica count only"", ""Set a longer terminationGracePeriodSeconds"", ""Use a Recreate strategy"""]",0,"Topology spread constraints and anti-affinity distribute replicas so a single node loss cannot take out every replica. Raising replica count helps capacity but can put them all on one node, so it does not address correlated failure.",MEDIUM,availability
kubernetes,"What does kube-proxy do?","[""Programs node networking rules so Service virtual IPs reach the backing pods"", ""Assigns pod IP addresses"", ""Schedules pods onto nodes"", ""Resolves DNS names for services"""]",0,"kube-proxy watches Services and EndpointSlices and programs iptables or IPVS rules so the ClusterIP is load balanced to pod IPs. IP assignment is the CNI's job and DNS is CoreDNS's, which are separate components often confused with it.",EASY,networking
kubernetes,"Which command streams logs from all pods in a deployment, including previous crashed instances?","[""kubectl logs deployment/<name> --all-pods=true --prefix"", ""kubectl logs deployment/<name>"", ""kubectl describe deployment/<name>"", ""kubectl top deployment/<name>"""]",0,"--all-pods=true reads logs from every pod in the deployment, which matters when the container is crash looping and the current instance has produced nothing. --prefix labels which pod each line came from.",MEDIUM,troubleshooting
aws,"Placeholder row for parsing coverage","[""a"", ""b"", ""c"", ""d"""]",0,"unused",HARD,unused
`;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FILES["terraform"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
terraform,"What does terraform plan show you that terraform apply then performs?","[""A proposed diff of create, update, destroy and replace actions"", ""The exact API calls that will be made"", ""A validation of HCL syntax only"", ""The current state of every resource"""]",0,"plan compares configuration, state and reality and reports the actions required. It does not show the exact API calls, which is why an apply can still fail on something outside Terraform's control such as a quota or a permissions change.",EASY,workflow
terraform,"Why must remote state be stored with encryption and locking?","[""State holds plaintext secrets and concurrent applies can corrupt it"", ""Remote state is slower than local state"", ""Locking prevents accidental destroys"", ""Encryption improves plan accuracy"""]",0,"Providers persist every attribute into state, including passwords and tokens. Locking prevents two engineers applying at once and overwriting each other's view of reality. Neither is optional for a shared backend.",MEDIUM,state
terraform,"What does the '# forces replacement' marker in a plan mean?","[""Changing that attribute cannot be done in place, so the resource will be destroyed and recreated"", ""The resource will be updated in place"", ""The plan cannot proceed without input"", ""The provider version is too old"""]",0,"Some attributes are immutable after creation, such as an EC2 instance's subnet or an EBS volume's Availability Zone. Replacement risks downtime and can orphan a public IP or an Elastic IP, so treat those lines as breaking changes.",MEDIUM,lifecycle
terraform,"What is the difference between count and for_each on a resource?","[""count takes a number and indexes from zero; for_each takes a set or map and keys by value, which is stable"", ""They are interchangeable"", ""for_each is deprecated"", ""count supports maps, for_each does not"""]",0,"count's keys are positional, so removing an item from the middle of a list shifts every later index and forces replacement. for_each keys by the given key, so removing one item affects only that instance. Prefer for_each whenever the keys are meaningful.",MEDIUM,count
terraform,"What does the sensitive attribute on a resource actually do?","[""Redacts the value in CLI output; the value is still written to state and plan files in plaintext"", ""Encrypts the value in state"", ""Prevents the value from being sent to the provider"", ""Validates the value's format"""]",0,"The flag only suppresses display. The value persists in state and in saved plan output, so the real fix is keeping secrets out of Terraform entirely and injecting them at runtime from Secrets Manager or SSM. Marking a resource sensitive also just makes it harder to debug.",HARD,sensitive
terraform,"When should you use terraform state mv?","[""After refactoring module structure, to re-point state at the same real resource"", ""To move a resource between AWS accounts"", ""To migrate a resource between regions"", ""To rename a variable"""]",0,"state mv updates the state mapping without destroying or recreating anything, which is exactly what you need after reorganising modules. state rm forgets a resource so Terraform stops managing it. Never hand-edit state.",MEDIUM,state
terraform,"What is Terraform drift, and what is the right response?","[""Real resources changed outside Terraform; investigate the cause before applying, since apply would revert the change"", ""The provider version is outdated"", ""The state file is corrupt"", ""A module failed to load"""]",0,"During an incident someone fixes something in the console, and the next plan wants to revert it. Applying blindly would undo the fix, so find out who changed what and why, then either import the change or bring the code in line.",HARD,drift
terraform,"What does create_before_destroy do, and when is it required?","[""Creates the replacement before destroying the original, required when two of the same resource cannot coexist"", ""Reuses the existing resource"", ""Creates resources in a different region"", ""Prevents the resource from being destroyed"""]",0,"Needed for resources that cannot overlap, such as a security group on an ENI, a database with a fixed name, or a DNS record with the same name. It is often paired with a unique name and sometimes a depends_on to force ordering.",HARD,lifecycle
terraform,"Why pin provider versions with required_providers?","[""To keep configuration reproducible and avoid a provider upgrade turning a green apply red"", ""To speed up terraform init"", ""To prevent providers making API calls"", ""To encrypt the state file"""]",0,"Without a version constraint, init can pull a provider with breaking behaviour. Commit the lock file so local, CI and production resolve identically, and let Renovate or Dependabot bump versions on a schedule.",EASY,providers
terraform,"What happens when a module variable has no default and the caller does not set it?","[""Terraform prompts interactively, or errors in automation"", ""It defaults to null and the resource is skipped"", ""Terraform infers a value"", ""The module is skipped"""]",0,"A variable without a default is required. In a terminal Terraform prompts; in CI there is no terminal, so it fails with a clear message. Always add type constraints too, since they catch wrong values at validate time rather than apply time.",EASY,modules
terraform,"What is the recommended pattern for sharing one S3 backend across many small configurations?","[""One state key per configuration, isolated by path, with locking enabled"", ""A single shared state key for all of them"", ""Local state committed to a private repo"", ""A separate AWS account per configuration with no backend"""]",0,"Each configuration needs its own state, achieved with distinct keys or Terraform Cloud workspaces. Sharing one key causes conflicting writes and lock contention, which is a genuinely painful failure to debug.",MEDIUM,backend
terraform,"What does a lifecycle ignore_changes block do?","[""Tells Terraform to stop detecting drift on the listed attributes"", ""Skips the resource during plan"", ""Forces the resource to be replaced"", ""Ignores errors during apply"""]",0,"ignore_changes suppresses drift detection for the listed attributes so Terraform leaves them alone. It is appropriate when something else legitimately owns the attribute, such as a default VPC setting or a tag mutated by an autoscaler, but it is also the easy way to hide a real bug, so use it deliberately.",HARD,lifecycle
terraform,"What does terraform validate do?","[""Checks configuration syntax and internal consistency without contacting providers"", ""Shows the planned changes"", ""Applies the configuration"", ""Refreshes the state file"""]",0,"validate catches type errors, bad references and invalid argument shapes locally, with no API calls, so it is fast and belongs in a pre-commit or CI step. It will not catch things that only surface against real infrastructure, which is why plan matters too.",EASY,workflow
terraform,"Why is it a problem to hardcode a value that also exists as a computed attribute?","[""It becomes a permanent diff because Terraform tries to correct the value every plan"", ""Terraform cannot handle computed values"", ""It causes the apply to fail"", ""It disables state locking"""]",0,"If you set an attribute the provider computes, every plan shows a change from the real value to your literal. The fix is to omit the argument and let it be computed, or to reference the resource attribute rather than hardcoding it.",MEDIUM,best-practices
terraform,"What does the depends_on meta-argument control?","[""Explicit ordering between resources that have no implicit dependency through references"", ""Which provider to use"", ""Whether a resource is created or destroyed"", ""The order in which variables are evaluated"""]",0,"Terraform infers ordering from references, which is nearly always what you want. depends_on is for the cases with no reference relationship, such as forcing an IAM policy attachment before a role is assumed. It over-couples resources, so reach for it only when necessary.",MEDIUM,best-practices
terraform,"What is the difference between a count-indexed resource address and a for_each-keyed one, in terms of state?","[""Count addresses are positional, so reordering forces replacement; for_each addresses are stable across changes"", ""They behave identically"", ""for_each cannot be used with modules"", ""Count addresses are stored in a separate file"""]",0,"The address is how Terraform identifies a resource instance in state. Positional addresses mean a list edit is a destroy and create; keyed addresses mean only the changed key is touched. This is the single biggest source of accidental replacements.",MEDIUM,count
terraform,"Which command is appropriate for importing existing infrastructure into Terraform management?","[""terraform import, or an import block, which previews then applies"", ""terraform apply -import"", ""terraform state add"", ""terraform refresh"""]",0,"terraform import brings one existing resource under management; an import block does the same inside configuration, running plan first so you can see what it would do. Hand-editing state to add a resource is not supported.",EASY,workflow
terraform,"What does a module output actually do?","[""Exposes a value from the child module to the caller"", ""Prints a value during apply"", ""Writes a value to a file"", ""Declares a variable with a default"""]",0,"Outputs are the module's public interface, and a module without outputs is unusable to its caller. Marking an output sensitive keeps it out of plan output but, as with resources, the value is still in state.",EASY,modules
terraform,"What is the effect of a remote state backend with a DynamoDB or S3 lock table?","[""It serialises applies so two runs cannot corrupt each other's state"", ""It encrypts the state file"", ""It speeds up plan"", ""It validates provider credentials"""]",0,"Locking is about concurrency: without it, two applies can both read the same state and the second write loses the first's changes. Encryption is configured separately with server-side encryption on the bucket.",MEDIUM,state
terraform,"Why should base images be pinned by digest rather than tag in an infrastructure pipeline?","[""Tags are mutable, so two builds of the same commit can differ"", ""Digests compress better"", ""Tags cannot be used with caching"", ""Digests enable multi-architecture images"""]",0,"A tag like node:22 points wherever it currently resolves, so a green build can turn red with no code change. Pinning the digest and bumping it on a schedule gives reproducible builds with a controlled update path.",MEDIUM,reproducibility
terraform,"What happens when you delete a resource from your Terraform configuration?","[""Terraform plans to destroy it, since state still tracks it"", ""Terraform ignores it"", ""Terraform reports an error"", ""Terraform imports it automatically"""]",0,"Removing a block from code does not remove the resource from state, so the next plan shows a destroy. To stop managing a resource without destroying it, use terraform state rm or removed blocks in configuration.",MEDIUM,state
terraform,"What does an assume_role provider configuration do?","[""Makes the provider authenticate as a different role, usually for cross-account access"", ""Assigns the provider to a different region"", ""Encrypts provider requests"", ""Caches provider credentials"""]",0,"An assume_role block generates temporary credentials for another role via STS, which is how you manage resources in a different account or with different permissions. Combine with a role_arn plus external_id and a session duration for cross-account setups.",MEDIUM,providers
terraform,"Why can terraform apply succeed on a second run and fail on the first?","[""The first run creates dependent resources such as IAM policies that take time to propagate"", ""Terraform is nondeterministic"", ""The provider caches credentials"", ""State was locked"""]",0,"Eventual consistency in AWS is a real source of first-apply failures, especially IAM policy propagation. If this is structural rather than occasional, model the dependency explicitly with a data source or an explicit wait, rather than relying on retries.",HARD,gotchas
terraform,"What is the correct way to reference a resource attribute from another resource?","[""By its address and attribute, which also creates an implicit ordering dependency"", ""By its name only"", ""By its creation timestamp"", ""By querying the API directly"""]",0,"Referencing an attribute is what tells Terraform the ordering, so you rarely need depends_on. Naming the resource without an attribute creates no dependency and no ordering, which is a common bug when a value is needed but not referenced.",MEDIUM,best-practices
terraform,"What does the -target flag do, and what is the risk?","[""Applies only the targeted resource and its dependencies, which can leave state inconsistent"", ""Applies only resources in the given file"", ""Runs plan faster"", ""Skips the provider configuration"""]",0,"-target is a tempting way to unblock a broken plan, but the result is a state that does not reflect a real apply, and a later full apply can produce surprising changes. Treat it as a last resort for recovery, not a routine workflow.",MEDIUM,workflow
terraform,"What is a reasonable way to version and roll out module changes across a large configuration set?","[""Use a versioned module source or git ref, and adopt the new version in controlled increments"", ""Edit every caller at once without review"", ""Fork the module per team permanently"", ""Pin to the main branch"""]",0,"Versioned module sources or git refs make adoption explicit and reviewable. Pinning to a moving branch means every caller picks up unreviewed changes at unpredictable times, which is how a shared module causes an outage.",MEDIUM,modules
terraform,"Why does Terraform record sensitive values in state even when marked sensitive?","[""Providers need them to manage the resource, so they are stored regardless of display"", ""Sensitive is only a display hint for the CLI and does not affect storage"", ""They are encrypted with the provider's key"", ""They are omitted from state entirely"""]",0,"Two things are true: the flag is a display hint, and providers persist attributes so they can diff and destroy resources later. The practical answer is to keep secrets out of Terraform and source them at runtime, not to rely on the flag for protection.",HARD,sensitive
terraform,"What does the -json output flag enable?","[""Machine-readable plan output for policy checks, CI gating and dashboards"", ""Compact human-readable output"", ""Importing existing resources"", ""Encrypting the plan file"""]",0,"Plan -json gives structured output that policy-as-code tools and CI can evaluate, for example blocking a plan that makes a database public. It is the basis for most modern Terraform governance workflows.",EASY,workflow
terraform,"When would you use a data source instead of a resource?","[""To read something that already exists, so Terraform references it rather than creating it"", ""To create a resource with different settings"", ""To refresh an existing managed resource"", ""To import an existing resource into state"""]",0,"A data source reads existing infrastructure without managing its lifecycle. A resource creates and manages. Using a resource for something Terraform should not own is how you end up fighting a tool that keeps reverting someone else's change.",EASY,data-sources
`;

