FILES["linux"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
linux,"What does a process state of D mean?","[""Uninterruptible disk sleep, waiting on I/O"", ""Stopped by a debugger"", ""Sleeping in an interruptible wait"", ""Zombie process"""]",0,"D is uninterruptible sleep, usually waiting on storage or NFS. It cannot be killed until the I/O completes, which is why D-state processes survive kill -9. A flood of D-state processes pointing at an unresponsive NFS mount is a classic cause of a wedged host.",HARD,processes
linux,"Which signal cannot be caught or ignored?","[""SIGKILL"", ""SIGTERM"", ""SIGINT"", ""SIGHUP"""]",0,"SIGKILL and SIGSTOP are the only two the kernel does not let a process intercept. SIGKILL is used when termination must be guaranteed, but it leaves no chance to clean up, so prefer SIGTERM and escalate only if needed.",MEDIUM,signals
linux,"What does the setgid bit on a directory do?","[""New files inherit the directory's group rather than the creator's primary group"", ""Makes new files inherit the directory's permissions"", ""Prevents deletion by the owner"", ""Allows giving away the file"""]",0,"chmod g+s on a directory makes new files and subdirectories take the directory's group, which is how shared project directories let a whole team edit files. On a regular file, setgid means executable as the group owner, which is what sudo uses.",MEDIUM,permissions
linux,"Why can a filesystem report 'No space left on device' while df -h shows free space?","[""Inodes are exhausted, which df -i reveals"", ""The filesystem is corrupted"", ""df is reporting in different units"", ""The root reserve blocks the write"""]",0,"df -h measures data blocks and df -i measures inodes, and they fill at different rates. A directory holding hundreds of thousands of tiny files exhausts inodes while the block count stays low. Removing the small files frees inodes immediately.",MEDIUM,disk
linux,"What does kill -9 send?","[""SIGKILL, which the kernel enforces immediately"", ""SIGTERM with a nine second delay"", ""SIGKILL after SIGTERM"", ""SIGSTOP"""]",0,"kill sends SIGTERM (15), which a well-behaved service uses to drain and flush before exiting. kill -9 sends SIGKILL (9), enforced at once, so temp files and half-written state are left behind. In systemd, systemctl stop sends SIGTERM and only escalates after TimeoutStopSec.",EASY,signals
linux,"Should you be concerned when free memory on a Linux host is near zero?","[""No; free memory is used for page cache, so check available memory and swap activity"", ""Yes, the system will crash"", ""Yes, it indicates a memory leak"", ""No, because Linux ignores unused RAM"""]",0,"Linux deliberately fills spare RAM with page cache and reclaims it under pressure, so low free with healthy available is normal and good. The signals that matter are available dropping, swap being used, and si/so in vmstat showing active swapping. In containers, watch the cgroup limit rather than the host.",MEDIUM,memory
linux,"Which command shows which process holds a TCP port?","[""ss -tlnp"", ""ps aux"", ""top -u nobody"", ""free -h"""]",0,"ss -tlnp lists listening sockets with the process name and PID, and needs root to see other users' processes. lsof -i :8080 is the classic equivalent, and netstat -tlnp is the fallback on minimal images that lack ss.",EASY,networking
linux,"What does '?' in the TTY column of ps output indicate?","[""The process has no controlling terminal"", ""The process is a zombie"", ""The process is being traced"", ""The process runs in a different session"""]",0,"No controlling terminal is typical of daemons and kernel threads. It is a quick way to spot user-initiated processes in a list otherwise dominated by services.",MEDIUM,processes
linux,"Why does a container get exit code 137 even though the host has free memory?","[""It exceeded its cgroup memory limit and was OOM-killed"", ""The host ran out of memory"", ""The image pull failed"", ""It exceeded its CPU share"""]",0,"Containers are bounded by cgroup limits independent of host capacity, so a process can be killed with the host showing plenty free. Check docker stats for usage against the container's limit, and set the limit explicitly rather than inheriting an unknown one.",MEDIUM,containers
linux,"What does status=203/EXEC mean in a systemd unit failure?","[""The ExecStart command could not be executed"", ""The unit file is malformed"", ""The service exited non-zero"", ""A dependency is missing"""]",0,"203/EXEC specifically means systemd could not execute the binary. Common causes: a wrong absolute path, CRLF line endings from a file edited on Windows, a missing shebang, or a mount with noexec. systemctl status plus journalctl -u gives the precise message.",HARD,systemd
linux,"Which command shows live HTTP headers during a request?","[""tcpdump -A"", ""tcpdump -X"", ""tcpdump -v"", ""tcpdump -nn -c 100"""]",0,"-A prints the payload as ASCII so HTTP headers are readable. -X shows hex and ASCII, useful for binary content. Always add -nn to avoid slow reverse lookups and -i any to capture across interfaces.",MEDIUM,networking
linux,"How do you find which files a running process has open?","[""lsof -p <pid>"", ""strace -p <pid>"", ""ps -ef"", ""top -p <pid>"""]",0,"lsof -p lists open file descriptors, including sockets and the current working directory, which often spots a process writing somewhere unexpected. strace -p attaches a syscall tracer and shows exactly which paths it tries to open, which pinpoints the cause when lsof only shows the symptom.",MEDIUM,debugging
linux,"Why does a union filesystem mean deleting a large file in a RUN step does not shrink an image?","[""Each RUN is a new layer and image size sums all layers, including the deleted data"", ""rm does not work inside images"", ""The base image layer is compressed"", ""Docker deduplicates at runtime"""]",0,"Layers are additive and never shrink, so apt lists downloaded in layer N persist even after deletion in N+1. The fix is combining install and cleanup in one RUN with &&, or using multi-stage builds.",MEDIUM,containers
linux,"What does vmstat show that top does not?","[""Block I/O, context switches and swap in/out activity"", ""Per-process CPU usage"", ""Memory usage only"", ""Network throughput"""]",0,"vmstat gives the system-level view: si and so show active swapping, bi and bo show block I/O, and the context switch rate shows scheduler pressure. That is what distinguishes a CPU problem from an I/O or memory-thrash problem.",MEDIUM,performance
linux,"What is the difference between a soft and a hard link to a file?","[""A soft link is a path that points at a name; a hard link is another directory entry for the same inode"", ""They are identical"", ""A hard link can cross filesystems, a soft link cannot"", ""A soft link cannot point at directories"""]",0,"A hard link shares the inode, so deleting the original does not free the data while any hard link remains, but it cannot cross filesystems and cannot point at a directory. A symlink stores a path, survives the target being renamed, and can cross filesystems, but breaks if the target is deleted.",HARD,filesystem
linux,"Why does a running system ignore changes made to /etc/fstab until a reboot or mount -a?","[""The kernel and systemd cache the mount table; mount -a applies it without rebooting"", ""fstab is only read at boot by design"", ""systemd never monitors it"", ""It does; a reload is always required first"""]",0,"fstab is read at boot and by systemd at daemon-reload. mount -a applies any new or changed entries immediately, and systemctl daemon-reload picks up unit changes. Rebooting is a common but unnecessary way to apply a mount change.",MEDIUM,systemd
linux,"What does a zombie process indicate?","[""Its parent has not reaped it after it exited"", ""The process is sleeping on I/O"", ""The process has been stopped"", ""The process is out of memory"""]",0,"A zombie has already exited and is only holding a PID slot, because the parent has not called wait(). The fix is in the parent, typically a process that does not reap children, so restarting the parent resolves it. A handful is harmless; thousands means a PID leak.",MEDIUM,processes
linux,"Which command shows the open file descriptor limit for a process?","[""cat /proc/<pid>/limits"", ""ulimit -a"", ""ls -l /proc/<pid>/fd"", ""df -i"""]",0,"/proc/<pid>/limits shows the actual limits in force for that process, including open files. ulimit -a shows the current shell's limits, which may not match what a running daemon inherited. ls -l /proc/<pid>/fd lists descriptors but not the limits themselves.",MEDIUM,limits
linux,"What does strace attach to and show?","[""System calls made by a process, which pinpoints where a failure originates"", ""Memory usage over time"", ""Network packets"", ""The process's environment"""]",0,"strace shows syscalls such as open, read and connect, which is how you find out a process is trying to open a path that does not exist or retrying a connection that is refused. Attach with -p and filter with -e to keep the output readable.",MEDIUM,debugging
linux,"Why can a systemd unit fail with 'Failed to start ... because the control process exited'?","[""ExecStart ran but the process exited non-zero, often after failing its own startup checks"", ""The binary is missing"", ""The unit file has a syntax error"", ""The service has no dependencies"""]",0,"That message means the process started and then exited non-zero, as opposed to 203/EXEC which means it could not start at all. journalctl -u <unit> -e shows the process's own error output, which is usually the actual cause.",MEDIUM,systemd
linux,"What is checked first when diagnosing high load average with low CPU utilisation across all cores?","[""Processes blocked in uninterruptible I/O wait, shown as D state"", ""The load average calculation itself is wrong"", ""CPU frequency scaling"", ""The number of running processes is always accurate"""]",0,"Load average counts runnable and uninterruptible tasks, so it can be high while CPU sits idle. That combination almost always means storage or NFS latency, and the processes in D state are the ones to look at.",HARD,performance
linux,"How do environment variables get into a systemd service?","[""From Environment= directives in the unit file or a drop-in, not from your interactive shell"", ""Inherited automatically from the shell that ran systemctl"", ""Read from ~/.bashrc at service start"", ""Passed as command-line arguments"""]",0,"Services do not inherit the interactive environment, which is the usual reason a variable works in your shell but is empty in the service. Use Environment= or EnvironmentFile= in a drop-in under /etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/, then systemctl daemon-reload.",MEDIUM,systemd
linux,"What does ss -s or netstat -s summarise?","[""Socket statistics, including errors and retransmits"", ""Routing table contents"", ""Listening ports only"", ""ARP cache entries"""]",0,"Summarised socket counters show cumulative errors, resets and retransmits, which helps distinguish a network problem from an application problem quickly. Compare the deltas over an interval rather than the totals, which are usually high on a long-running host.",MEDIUM,networking
linux,"Why does deleting an open file not free its disk space immediately?","[""The inode stays allocated until the last file descriptor is closed"", ""The filesystem caches deletions"", ""The file is moved to a temp directory"", ""deletion is asynchronous"""]",0,"The directory entry is removed but the inode and blocks are held until the last open descriptor closes. This is why you can free space on a running service by deleting a large file the service is still writing to, and why the space reappears if the service reopens it.",HARD,filesystem
linux,"What does the -9 flag's absence in kill -TERM imply about process shutdown?","[""The process is given time to handle the signal and clean up before the kernel acts"", ""The kernel kills it immediately regardless"", ""The signal is sent to the process group"", ""The process is suspended rather than terminated"""]",0,"SIGTERM is a request: a well-written process closes listeners, finishes in-flight work and exits. Giving it a grace period before SIGKILL is what prevents dropped connections and half-written state during a restart.",EASY,signals
linux,"Which command shows which filesystem a given file is on?","[""df -h <path>"", ""du -sh <path>"", ""ls -l <path>"", ""mount <path>"""]",0,"df reports usage per filesystem, so it answers which filesystem and how full. du reports the space a file or directory occupies, which is a different question. For a path that is not a mount point, df walks up to the containing filesystem.",EASY,disk
linux,"What is the cause of 'cannot allocate memory' when free reports plenty available?","[""A limit on virtual address space or a process count limit, not physical memory"", ""The page cache is too large"", ""swap is corrupted"", ""The kernel version is too old"""]",0,"Allocation failures often come from limits rather than exhaustion: RLIMIT_AS, a cgroup memory limit, or overcommit settings. Check /proc/<pid>/limits and the cgroup limit for the service rather than trusting free.",HARD,memory
linux,"What does iostat -x 1 most directly tell you?","[""Per-device utilisation, await and queue depth, which separates slow IOPS from slow latency"", ""Overall CPU percentage"", ""Network throughput"", ""Which processes are using disk"""]",0,"-x adds the extended fields: %util, await and aqu-sz. High util with low await points at saturation; low util with high await points at slow media or high queue depth elsewhere. Missing await is why plain iostat often misleads.",HARD,performance
linux,"When should you use update-alternatives or a version manager rather than editing PATH?","[""When multiple versions of the same tool are installed and you need to switch the default"", ""When a binary is not on PATH at all"", ""When permissions are wrong"", ""When the shell is not bash"""]",0,"update-alternatives manages symlinks for system-wide defaults on Debian-family systems, which survives package upgrades. Editing PATH in a profile works per user but does not help daemons, which do not read your shell profile.",MEDIUM,packages
linux,"What does the exit status 127 from a shell script indicate?","[""Command not found"", ""Permission denied"", ""The last command returned 127"", ""Segmentation fault"""]",0,"127 conventionally means the command could not be found, and 126 means found but not executable, commonly a permission or noexec problem. Both are shell conventions rather than kernel guarantees, but they are reliable enough to branch on in scripts.",MEDIUM,scripting
`;

