FILES["networking"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
networking,"In what order does a typical HTTPS request resolve and connect?","[""DNS, then TCP, then TLS, then HTTP"", ""TCP, then DNS, then TLS, then HTTP"", ""TLS, then DNS, then TCP, then HTTP"", ""DNS, then HTTP, then TLS, then TCP"""]",0,"The client resolves the name, establishes TCP with a three-way handshake, negotiates TLS, and only then sends the HTTP request. Each step is a place latency accrues, which is why connection reuse and session resumption matter. HTTP/3 over QUIC folds transport and TLS together but still needs an address first.",EASY,tls
networking,"What does a DNS TTL control?","[""How long resolvers may cache the record, which bounds how fast a change propagates"", ""The lifetime of the DNS server process"", ""How long a TLS certificate is cached"", ""The maximum queries per second"""]",0,"TTL sets the cache lifetime, so a high TTL slows failover and a low one speeds it at the cost of more queries. Some resolvers and ISPs ignore short TTLs and cache longer, so failover time is a floor rather than a guarantee, and client-side caches are separate again.",EASY,dns
networking,"What is the three-way TCP handshake?","[""SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, requiring a full round trip before data flows"", ""SYN then data, with no acknowledgement"", ""A UDP-style connection with retries"", ""A TLS concept"""]",0,"The client sends SYN, the server replies SYN-ACK, the client sends ACK, and only then can data flow. That is one full round trip before the first byte of the request, so every additional hop adds real latency and connection reuse matters proportionally more on long paths.",MEDIUM,tcp
networking,"How many usable host addresses does a /28 provide in an AWS VPC?","[""11, because AWS reserves five"", ""14"", ""16"", ""12"""]",0,"A /28 leaves 4 host bits, giving 16 addresses, and AWS reserves all five of the first and last: network address, the VPC router, DNS at .2, future use, and broadcast. On a traditional network only two are reserved, giving 14, and this asymmetry catches people out in VPC design.",MEDIUM,cidr
networking,"What is MTU and how does it relate to a common packet-drop problem?","[""MTU is the largest frame an interface sends; encapsulation overhead can exceed the path MTU and cause silent drops"", ""MTU is the number of concurrent connections"", ""MTU is the timeout before a packet is retried"", ""MTU applies only to UDP"""]",0,"MTU is 1500 on Ethernet; MSS is MTU minus headers, roughly 1460 for IPv4. Tunnels and overlays add headers, and if the result exceeds the path MTU with Don't Fragment set, routers drop it silently. The symptom is a connection that works for small requests and hangs on large ones; the fix is clamping MSS or lowering the interface MTU.",HARD,mtu
networking,"What does a layer 4 load balancer do that a layer 7 one cannot?","[""Forward by port while preserving the client IP, with less protocol parsing and lower latency"", ""Route by URL path"", ""Terminate TLS"", ""Support sticky sessions"""]",0,"An L4 balancer moves packets by port, so it preserves the source address and handles very high throughput, but it cannot inspect HTTP. An L7 balancer can route by path or header and terminate TLS, at the cost of more CPU and latency.",MEDIUM,load-balancing
networking,"What is connection tracking and why does exhausting it break services?","[""The kernel tracks flow state; a full table drops new connections, often after a scan or spike"", ""It balances new connections across backends"", ""It encrypts established connections"", ""It compresses TCP headers"""]",0,"conntrack holds an entry per flow across the firewall. When the table fills, the kernel refuses to create new entries, presenting as random connection failures under load. Raising nf_conntrack_max and the buckets is the direct fix, and it is a common cause of mysterious intermittent failures on NAT or load-balancer instances.",HARD,conntrack
networking,"Why does asymmetric routing break stateful firewalls?","[""Return traffic takes a different path, so the firewall has no state for the flow"", ""The firewall cannot handle high throughput"", ""Asymmetric routes are blocked by BGP"", ""It only affects UDP"""]",0,"A stateful firewall expects both directions of a flow through the same device. If replies return via another router, the firewall sees them as unsolicited inbound and drops them. This is a classic multi-AZ pitfall when routing is not designed symmetrically.",HARD,troubleshooting
networking,"Which DNS record type maps a hostname directly to an IPv6 address?","[""AAAA"", ""A"", ""CNAME"", ""PTR"""]",0,"AAAA is the IPv6 equivalent of A. A CNAME aliases one name to another and cannot coexist with other record types at the same name, which is why the zone apex cannot be a CNAME. PTR is the reverse lookup record.",EASY,dns
networking,"What does DNSSEC provide that a TLS certificate does not?","[""Authenticated DNS data, preventing record tampering in transit"", ""Encryption of DNS queries"", ""Faster resolution"", ""Automatic certificate issuance"""]",0,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so a validating resolver can detect tampering, but it does not encrypt queries and it is not confidentiality. TLS on the connection is what protects the data; DNSSEC protects the name resolution step.",MEDIUM,dns
networking,"What causes a slow TLS handshake to be much worse for mobile clients than for a datacentre client?","[""Higher and more variable round-trip time, and per-connection session setup cost"", ""Mobile devices use older ciphers by default"", ""Mobile carriers block TLS 1.3"", ""Handshakes are cached only on wifi"""]",0,"Handshake cost is dominated by round trips, so latency multiplies it. TLS 1.3 with session resumption cuts this to roughly one round trip, and 0-RTT or connection coalescing cuts it further. Without resumption, a high-latency mobile client pays the full cost on every new connection.",MEDIUM,tls
networking,"Why can a route flap between availability zones cause application-level failures even when connectivity looks fine?","[""Each shift can reset in-flight TCP connections and shift traffic to unequal or degraded targets"", ""Flapping is a layer 2 problem only"", ""Flapping only affects UDP"", ""Flapping blocks DNS"""]",0,"Route flapping means traffic repeatedly shifts paths, and every shift can break in-flight TCP connections, remap clients onto cold caches, and create uneven load. It is often a symptom of asymmetric routing, which also breaks conntrack, so the two problems frequently appear together.",HARD,bgp
networking,"What is the effect of a lower DNS TTL before a planned migration?","[""Resolvers re-query sooner, so the change propagates faster at the cost of more queries"", ""It has no effect until a restart"", ""It only affects the primary nameserver"", ""It increases record resolution accuracy"""]",0,"Lower the TTL well before you need to move traffic, and raise it again afterwards to reduce query volume. Remember some resolvers and ISPs cache longer than the TTL, and client-side caches add their own delay, so this shortens the window without eliminating it.",EASY,dns
networking,"Which problem does HTTP/3 over QUIC specifically address?","[""Head-of-line blocking across streams at the transport layer, and loss of packets affecting unrelated streams"", ""Encryption overhead"", ""DNS latency"", ""Server certificate management"""]",0,"HTTP/2 fixed application-layer head-of-line blocking but kept it at the transport layer, where a lost TCP packet stalls every stream. QUIC runs over UDP with independent streams and per-stream loss recovery, so one lost packet does not block the others. It also allows 0-RTT resumption.",HARD,protocols
networking,"What does an anycast address do?","[""Advertises the same address from multiple locations so traffic goes to the nearest"", ""Assigns a unique address per client"", ""Balances traffic by hash"", ""Encrypts traffic at the edge"""]",0,"Anycast advertises one IP from many sites, and normal routing sends traffic to the closest, which is how DNS root servers and CDNs work. Unlike L4 load balancing, it has no awareness of connection state, so TCP connection migration requires QUIC.",MEDIUM,architecture
networking,"Why can a CNAME record not be placed at the apex of a DNS zone?","[""A CNAME cannot coexist with any other record type at the same name, and the apex requires SOA and NS"", ""APEX records are not supported by any resolver"", ""A CNAME must be a subdomain"", ""It causes resolution loops"""]",0,"The apex must carry SOA and NS records, and a CNAME cannot be combined with any other data at the same name. Providers work around this with ALIAS or ANAME records that resolve server-side, which is why apex CNAMEs often appear to work through a hosting provider but not on plain DNS.",MEDIUM,dns
networking,"What is a common cause of intermittent TLS handshake failures only on some mobile networks?","[""An incomplete certificate chain that clients cannot fetch because AIA fetching is blocked"", ""The server CPU is saturated"", ""The client uses HTTP/2"", ""The record has a low TTL"""]",0,"A server must send the leaf plus the intermediates needed to build a path, and should omit the root. If the intermediate is missing and the client cannot fetch it because access to the CA's URL is blocked, validation fails with no obvious error. Carrier networks commonly block such fetches.",HARD,tls
networking,"What does the SNI extension solve?","[""Allows multiple TLS certificates to be served from one IP by including the requested hostname in the handshake"", ""Encrypts the SNI field"", ""Compresses the certificate"", ""Speeds up DNS resolution"""]",0,"Without SNI, a client sends only an IP address, so a shared IP cannot know which certificate to present. SNI carries the hostname in the ClientHello. Its limitation is that it is not encrypted by default, which is what Encrypted Client Hello addresses.",MEDIUM,tls
networking,"Which two ranges cover all subnets in a 10.0.0.0/16 VPC CIDR?","[""10.0.0.0 to 10.0.255.255"", ""10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255"", ""10.0.0.1 to 10.0.0.254"", ""10.0.0.0 to 10.0.0.255"""]",0,"A /16 fixes the first 16 bits and lets the rest vary, giving 10.0.0.0 through 10.0.255.255. Reference the broad VPC CIDR in peering and gateway routes, but narrow subnet CIDRs in security groups, so that a wide rule does not quietly cover more than intended.",MEDIUM,cidr
networking,"What is the role of a NAT gateway in a VPC?","[""Translates private addresses to a public address so outbound traffic can return"", ""Encrypts traffic leaving the VPC"", ""Load-balances between instances"", ""Is required for VPC peering"""]",0,"NAT rewrites private sources to the gateway's Elastic IP and translates back on return traffic, using a port mapping table. Private subnets deliberately have no route to the internet gateway, so outbound needs a NAT gateway; inbound needs a public subnet with an Elastic IP or a load balancer in front of private targets.",MEDIUM,nat
networking,"Why does traceroute showing hops beyond the failure point not mean the problem is there?","[""Routers deprioritise ICMP TTL-exceeded responses, so hop N+3 may simply not reply"", ""Traceroute always adds hops"", ""Those hops are in the destination region"", ""It indicates a routing loop"""]",0,"When a router decrements the TTL to zero it sends an ICMP time-exceeded message, and generating that message is low priority. So a hop that fails to respond is not necessarily the hop that dropped the traffic. Look for the first hop from which probes stop being received entirely, and corroborate with MTR loss patterns rather than a single traceroute run.",HARD,diagnostics
networking,"What does MTR give you that a single traceroute does not?","[""Continuous per-hop statistics over time, so loss patterns are visible rather than inferred"", ""The path only"", ""Encrypted probes"", ""Reverse DNS"""]",0,"MTR sends repeated probes and aggregates loss and latency per hop over a window, which distinguishes an intermittent drop from a hop that simply does not reply to ICMP. That distinction is exactly what a single traceroute run cannot make.",MEDIUM,diagnostics
networking,"What is the effect of enabling TCP keepalive without also configuring idle timeouts sensibly?","[""Connections can be reaped by a middlebox while both endpoints believe them open, causing silent stalls"", ""Keepalive increases bandwidth usage"", ""It disables connection pooling"", ""It forces HTTPS on all connections"""]",0,"Middleboxes drop idle connections, but the endpoints still consider them established. The first write after the gap stalls until a keepalive detects it. Either enable TCP keepalive with sensible intervals, or make the application-level idle timeout shorter than any middlebox's, which is why load balancers' keep-alive settings matter so much.",HARD,tcp
networking,"Why does a 502 from a load balancer specifically rule out client-side DNS?","[""The load balancer generated it, which means the request already reached the load balancer"", ""DNS is never involved in HTTP"", ""The client would show a different code"", ""502 is defined as a DNS error"""]",0,"A 502 is produced by the load balancer after accepting the TCP connection but failing to get a valid HTTP response from a target, so the client's name resolution and connectivity to the load balancer both demonstrably worked. A 503 means no healthy targets, a 504 a timeout. Establishing that first saves an hour of looking in the wrong place.",MEDIUM,troubleshooting
`;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FILES["docker"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
docker,"What does a multi-stage Dockerfile build allow you to do?","[""Build in a fat builder image and copy only the artefacts into a small runtime image"", ""Run one image in multiple architectures"", ""Run multiple containers from one image"", ""Cache dependencies across repositories"""]",0,"Multi-stage builds keep compilers, package managers and dev dependencies in a throwaway stage so the final image carries only the built output. A Go or Node build routinely shrinks from over a gigabyte to tens of megabytes, which directly cuts pull time and registry cost.",EASY,best-practices
docker,"What is the difference between ENTRYPOINT and CMD?","[""ENTRYPOINT is fixed and CMD supplies default arguments that can be overridden at run time"", ""ENTRYPOINT overrides CMD entirely"", ""CMD runs before ENTRYPOINT"", ""They are aliases"""]",0,"The final process is ENTRYPOINT followed by CMD, so with ENTRYPOINT [\"python\"] and CMD [\"app.py\"], passing an argument replaces CMD. A shell-form ENTRYPOINT runs through /bin/sh -c, which makes a shell PID 1 and breaks signal handling, so prefer the exec form.",MEDIUM,dockerfile
docker,"Why does nginx need the -g 'daemon off;' flag in a container?","[""So nginx stays the foreground PID 1 process"", ""To run it as a different user"", ""To enable IPv6"", ""To allow binding port 80 without root"""]",0,"A container lives as long as PID 1 runs. Without the flag nginx forks into the background and the container exits immediately, so the foreground flag is what makes the process lifecycle track the application.",MEDIUM,dockerfile
docker,"How do containers on the legacy default bridge network resolve each other by name?","[""They do not; automatic name resolution requires a user-defined network"", ""Docker's embedded DNS works on any bridge"", ""Names resolve via /etc/hosts automatically"", ""The host's DNS exposes container names"""]",0,"Docker's embedded DNS resolves service names only on user-defined bridge networks and Compose networks. The legacy default bridge lacks it, which is why the advice is always to create a named network, and why Compose sidesteps the issue by creating one.",MEDIUM,networking
docker,"What is the difference between a named volume and a bind mount?","[""A named volume is Docker-managed and survives container replacement; a bind mount maps a host path and suits source and config"", ""Bind mounts require root"", ""Named volumes cannot be shared"", ""Bind mounts only work on Linux"""]",0,"Named volumes are created and owned by Docker and survive deletion, which makes them right for databases and other stateful data. Bind mounts map arbitrary host paths and are ideal for live-reloading code or injecting config, but they let the container write to the host filesystem, which is a risk if the container is untrusted.",EASY,volumes
docker,"Why can a container fail to reach a database bound to localhost on the host?","[""127.0.0.1 inside a container is the container itself, so host.docker.internal or the host IP is needed"", ""Docker blocks all host traffic"", ""The container needs to be privileged"", ""localhost is not resolvable in containers"""]",0,"Loopback is per-network-namespace, so localhost inside a container refers to the container. On Docker Desktop, host.docker.internal is provided automatically; on Linux you add --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway. The database must also be bound to an address the container can reach, not just 127.0.0.1.",MEDIUM,networking
docker,"What does a Docker HEALTHCHECK do?","[""Runs a command periodically inside the container and reports healthy or unhealthy"", ""Verifies image layer integrity"", ""Checks the registry signature"", ""Scans for vulnerabilities"""]",0,"The healthcheck runs in the container and writes 0 or 1 to its status. Docker itself acts on it little, but orchestrators do: ECS marks the task unhealthy and can replace it, Swarm restarts it, and Kubernetes can map it to readiness and liveness. Without one, a container can be running and completely broken with nothing noticing.",MEDIUM,reliability
docker,"Why does using the legacy docker bridge with a privileged container pose a security risk?","[""The container has full network access to the host's interfaces rather than NAT isolation"", ""It runs as root by default"", ""It cannot access the internet"", ""It shares the host's filesystem"""]",0,"Containers on the default bridge are NAT'd, but a privileged container bypasses most isolation mechanisms at the kernel level, so together you get effectively host-level network and kernel access. Prefer user-defined networks with explicit egress rules and drop all unnecessary capabilities.",HARD,security
docker,"What does the exec form of a Docker ENTRYPOINT prevent that the shell form causes?","[""The shell wrapping the process, which would break signal delivery and PID 1 semantics"", ""The loss of environment variables"", ""The inability to set a working directory"", ""The loss of the image's CMD"""]",0,"Shell form runs /bin/sh -c \"<command>\", so the shell becomes PID 1 and the application is a child. Signals go to the shell, which may not forward them, so graceful shutdown never happens and zombie children accumulate. The exec form passes arguments directly to the binary.",MEDIUM,dockerfile
docker,"What is the effect of adding a file in a later Dockerfile layer after deleting it in an earlier one?","[""The file's bytes still exist in the earlier layer and contribute to image size"", ""The file is fully removed from the image"", ""Docker reclaims the space at build time"", ""It causes the build to fail"""]",0,"Layers are additive and immutable, so deleting a file in a later layer only hides it. Install and clean up in the same RUN joined with &&, or use multi-stage builds, and image size drops correspondingly.",MEDIUM,best-practices
docker,"Why can two containers on different Docker networks not resolve each other by name?","[""Each network has its own DNS namespace, and containers only share names within one network"", ""Docker only allows one network per host"", ""Name resolution requires both to be running"", ""The images must match"""]",0,"Docker's embedded DNS is scoped to a network. Containers on separate networks have separate namespaces and, critically, no route between them unless a container is attached to both. Declaring a single shared network is the fix.",MEDIUM,networking
docker,"What does --init or an init process in a container solve?","[""PID 1 does not reap orphaned child processes, so zombies accumulate"", ""The container cannot resolve DNS"", ""The image is too large"", ""Signals are not delivered"""]",0,"The kernel has special handling for PID 1 that skips default signal dispositions and child reaping, so orphaned children become zombies that never clear. Running tini or dumb-init as PID 1, or using --init, restores normal behaviour. This matters most in CI runners and any container that spawns subprocesses.",HARD,processes
docker,"What is the effect of running a container with the default bridge network and publishing a port?","[""Traffic is DNAT'd to the container, so the container does not see the client's real source IP"", ""The container receives the client's IP automatically"", ""Port publishing is disabled on the default bridge"", ""Only UDP can be published"""]",0,"On the default bridge, published ports are DNAT'd and the container sees the bridge gateway address rather than the client. With a user-defined bridge in ip-masquerade mode, or on host networking, the real source IP is preserved, which matters for logging and for IP-based access control.",HARD,networking
docker,"What is the difference between a build cache mount and baking a dependency into the image?","[""A cache mount reuses downloaded packages across builds without adding them to the image"", ""A cache mount makes builds faster but always invalidates"", ""They are interchangeable"", ""A cache mount only works in CI"""]",0,"With BuildKit, --mount=type=cache keeps downloaded dependencies outside the image entirely, so the layer never carries them and the cache can be shared across builds. Baking dependencies into an image is better when the runtime needs them, which is the case for OS packages but usually not for build tooling.",MEDIUM,best-practices
docker,"What does USER in a Dockerfile do beyond setting a name?","[""It sets the user for subsequent instructions and for the running process, so file ownership and permissions follow"", ""It only sets a label"", ""It grants root capabilities"", ""It changes the image's base OS"""]",0,"USER applies to the build context that follows and to the container's runtime user, which determines what the process can access. Running as a non-root user by default is one of the highest-value hardening steps for a container, since it limits the impact of a compromise.",MEDIUM,security
docker,"Why can a container report 'permission denied' writing to a mounted host directory it created files in?","[""The host directory's owner and the container's user often differ, and there is no mapping between them"", ""The volume is read-only"", ""The image lacks write permission bits"", ""Docker mounts directories read-only by default"""]",0,"Bind mounts carry host ownership through, so files created by a container user may be unwriteable by the host user and vice versa. Using named volumes, matching UID and GID, or --user fixes it. This is the most common cause of bind-mount write failures in development.",MEDIUM,volumes
`;

