FILES["cicd"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
cicd,"What is the key difference between a build artifact and a build cache?","[""An artifact is a promoted output whose integrity matters; a cache is disposable input used only for speed"", ""They are the same thing"", ""Caches are always encrypted"", ""Artifacts are stored on the runner"""]",0,"Artifacts are what you deploy, so they are versioned and integrity-checked. Caches are inputs like dependencies that merely save time and can be evicted at any moment. Treating a build output as a cache is a design smell: it makes builds slow to invalidate correctly and non-reproducible.",MEDIUM,pipelines
cicd,"Why should secrets be injected at runtime rather than baked into an image?","[""Image layers are easily inspected and persist in registries and caches even after the image is replaced"", ""Environment variables cannot hold secrets"", ""Images are compressed"", ""Runtime injection makes images immutable"""]",0,"A secret added by RUN or COPY becomes a layer anyone with pull access can read, and it survives in the registry and build caches even after a corrected image is pushed. Inject at runtime from a secret manager, and never use build args, since those are also visible in image history.",MEDIUM,secrets
cicd,"What does GitHub Actions OIDC replace?","[""Long-lived cloud keys stored as repository secrets, exchanged for short-lived role credentials"", ""The need for a self-hosted runner"", ""Docker layer caching"", ""Branch protection"""]",0,"With OIDC the workflow requests a signed identity token and the cloud exchanges it for brief credentials via an IAM role trust on the OIDC provider. The trust policy must constrain the sub claim to a specific repo and ref, otherwise any fork can assume the role, which is a widely exploited misconfiguration.",MEDIUM,security
cicd,"What is the main advantage of a canary deployment over blue/green?","[""A small share of traffic reaches the new version first, limiting blast radius while you watch real metrics"", ""It requires no monitoring"", ""It switches all traffic atomically"", ""It avoids the need for database migrations"""]",0,"A canary limits exposure: one percent of traffic means an undetected bug affects a small slice while you compare error rate and latency to baseline. Blue/green gets its safety from fast, reliable rollback rather than gradual exposure. Neither solves schema changes, which need expand-and-contract.",MEDIUM,deployments
cicd,"Why should a production database migration use expand-and-contract?","[""So old and new application versions can coexist during rollout, avoiding a window where either breaks"", ""So migrations run faster"", ""To avoid locking tables"", ""To make rollback automatic"""]",0,"Expand adds the new schema alongside the old and backfills, the deploy switches code to use it, and contract removes the old schema later once nothing references it. That is what makes a zero-downtime deploy possible when the schema must change, and it is the single most common cause of failed deploys when skipped.",HARD,migrations
cicd,"What is the right first response to a test that fails about one run in ten with no code change?","[""Quarantine and retry it to restore signal, then fix the shared state, timing or ordering causing it"", ""Delete the test"", ""Increase the pipeline timeout"", ""Disable parallel execution permanently"""]",0,"Quarantine stops the flake from training the team to ignore red, which is how flaky suites destroy pipeline trust. Then look for the usual causes: shared fixtures or database state, reliance on wall-clock time or random seeds, ordering dependencies, or tight timing assertions. Deleting the test loses coverage that may be catching something real.",MEDIUM,testing
cicd,"Why pin a base image by digest rather than by tag in production pipelines?","[""Tags are mutable, so the same commit can build differently on different days"", ""Digests compress better"", ""Tags cannot be cached"", ""Digests enable multi-arch images"""]",0,"A tag like node:22 points wherever it currently resolves, so two builds of the same commit can differ and a base image update can turn green into red with no code change. Pin the digest and let Renovate or Dependabot bump it on a schedule for reproducible builds with a controlled update path.",MEDIUM,reproducibility
cicd,"What does a pipeline gate requiring manual approval before a production deploy achieve?","[""A human checkpoint where the blast radius of an automatic deploy is high"", ""Faster pipelines"", ""Compile-time verification"", ""Replacement for automated testing"""]",0,"Manual gates suit risky, hard-to-reverse steps: production migrations, irreversible infrastructure changes, or releases during a freeze. Applied to every deploy they become a rubber stamp that trains the team to click through, so the value comes from being selective.",EASY,process
cicd,"Why can increasing CI parallelism make a test suite slower and flakier?","[""Parallel workers contend for shared resources and database state, increasing lock waits and timeouts"", ""Parallelism always slows builds"", ""Caches are invalidated in parallel builds"", ""It changes the exit code semantics"""]",0,"More workers on a fixed runner means each has less CPU and shares databases or caches, so contention produces timeouts that look like flakiness. Setting worker count from measured runner capacity rather than a percentage of an assumed core count is the practical fix.",MEDIUM,performance
cicd,"What does a rolling deployment strategy do during an update?","[""Replaces replicas gradually, creating new pods before removing old ones"", ""Tears down everything before starting"", ""Switches all traffic at once"", ""Scales to zero first"""]",0,"RollingUpdate creates new replicas and removes old ones in batches, keeping capacity throughout, which is why it is the default for Deployments. The cost is that both versions serve traffic simultaneously, which is exactly why schema changes need expand-and-contract.",MEDIUM,deployments
cicd,"What is the purpose of a pipeline's test stage ordering relative to the build stage?","[""Tests run against the built artifact, so the artifact that was tested is the artifact that ships"", ""Ordering is irrelevant"", ""Build must run after tests to have context"", ""Tests should run in parallel with the build"""]",0,"Building first and testing the resulting artifact removes the gap where tests validate something different from what is deployed. Testing source before build leaves a window where compilation or bundling introduces a problem. This is the core of build-once, promote-same-artifact practice.",MEDIUM,pipelines
cicd,"Why is storing build logs as artifacts useful beyond debugging failures?","[""They let you compare behaviour across environments and audit what happened in a release"", ""They make pipelines faster"", ""They replace test reports"", ""They reduce artifact storage cost"""]",0,"Retained logs let you diff behaviour between environments and reconstruct what happened during an incident, and they give an audit trail for compliance. They are usually configured with a shorter retention than build artifacts, since the artifacts themselves are the deployable output.",EASY,operations
cicd,"What does pipeline caching of a dependency directory need in order to be correct?","[""A key derived from the lockfile, so it invalidates exactly when dependencies change"", ""A key derived from the branch name only"", ""A unique key per run"", ""No key at all"""]",0,"Keying on the lockfile hash gives the fast path when nothing changed and correctness when something did. Keying on branch name alone serves stale dependencies and hides upstream changes, and a unique key per run provides no caching at all.",MEDIUM,caching
`;

