FILES["monitoring"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
monitoring,"What are the three Prometheus metric types and when is each used?","[""Counter for increasing events, Gauge for values that rise and fall, Histogram for distributions"", ""Counter, Gauge and Summary for distributions"", ""Integer, Float and String"", ""Counter, Gauge and Timer for wall clock"""]",0,"Counters only increase and suit rates like http_requests_total. Gauges move both ways and represent state such as queue depth. Histograms bucket observations so you can compute quantiles and rates of buckets, and unlike Summaries they aggregate correctly across instances, which is why Histogram is the default choice.",EASY,metrics
monitoring,"What do the RED and USE frameworks measure?","[""RED covers request rate, errors and duration for services; USE covers utilisation, saturation and errors for resources"", ""RED is for databases and USE is for HTTP"", ""They measure the same things in different orders"", ""USE is the newer name for RED"""]",0,"RED is the user-facing service view and USE is the constrained-resource view. Saturation is the interesting part of USE because utilisation can be high and fine right up until queues build, which is where latency actually degrades.",MEDIUM,frameworks
monitoring,"What is the most direct fix for a Prometheus instance exhausting memory due to ingest?","[""Drop high-cardinality labels such as user_id or request_id"", ""Increase the scrape interval to five minutes"", ""Switch gauges to counters"", ""Add more replicas"""]",0,"Prometheus stores a series per unique label combination, so an unbounded label multiplies series count and is the usual cause of high memory and slow queries. Drop it from metrics and route that dimension to logs or traces instead. More replicas do not reduce per-replica memory.",MEDIUM,cardinality
monitoring,"What is an SLO error budget and how should it guide decisions?","[""The permitted unreliability, which you may spend on releases; exhausting it means prioritising reliability"", ""A fixed monetary budget for the observability stack"", ""The number of alerts allowed per week"", ""The per-request time budget"""]",0,"An error budget of 0.1% on a 99.9% SLO is the unreliability available to spend. While budget remains, shipping features is correct; once exhausted, reliability work takes priority. This replaces the 'move fast versus move carefully' argument with an objective number.",MEDIUM,slo
monitoring,"What makes an alert actionable rather than noise?","[""A clear owner, a user-impact or SLO-burn threshold, and a runbook link"", ""Firing as often as possible"", ""Alerting on every warning log line"", ""Duplicating across PagerDuty and Slack"""]",0,"An actionable alert names what is broken for users, who owns it and what to do. Page on symptoms and burn rate rather than causes, because cause-based alerts fire for things not yet hurting anyone. Runbook links and a routing policy are what separate a page from a notification.",MEDIUM,alerting
monitoring,"What are the four golden signals?","[""Latency, traffic, errors and saturation"", ""CPU, memory, disk and network"", ""Availability, durability, consistency and partition tolerance"", ""Mean, median, mode and standard deviation"""]",0,"From the Google SRE book, these four generalise across services, queues and databases, which is why they work as a starting point before you tailor RED or USE to a specific system. Saturation is the one most often omitted and most predictive of future pain.",EASY,sre
monitoring,"Why does a high-cardinality alert threshold matter for Prometheus?","[""An unbounded label can grow series count until queries and memory degrade"", ""It reduces ingest cost only"", ""It changes the scrape interval"", ""It improves alert accuracy"""]",0,"A metric with user_id or request_id creates a new series per value, and Prometheus holds all of them in memory. Beyond a threshold the whole instance degrades, so cardinality budgets and label allowlists are an operational necessity rather than an optimisation.",HARD,cardinality
monitoring,"What does a burn-rate alert on an SLO do differently from alerting on raw error percentage?","[""It alerts on how fast the error budget is being consumed, which correlates with user pain"", ""It uses a lower threshold"", ""It requires a longer evaluation window"", ""It only works with multi-window alerts"""]",0,"Burn rate alerts use multi-window, multi-burn-rate configurations so they fire fast when consumption is rapid and stay quiet for slow burns that would otherwise cause alert fatigue. Raw error percentage has no notion of how much budget exists, so the same number means different things for a 99% and a 99.99% SLO.",HARD,slo
monitoring,"What is the difference between a counter-based rate and a gauge-based average for request rate?","[""Rates from counters are correct across resets and scaling; averaging a gauge can hide or distort the rate"", ""They are interchangeable"", ""Gauges cannot be used for rate calculations"", ""Counters require a recording rule"""]",0,"A rate over a counter is computed as the increase over a window divided by the window, which handles counter resets and instance scaling correctly. Averaging a per-instance gauge over instances weights by instance count rather than traffic, so a busy and an idle instance are treated equally, which is wrong.",HARD,metrics
monitoring,"Why is log volume often the largest cost driver in an observability stack?","[""Logs are unstructured and high-volume, with retention usually far longer than metrics"", ""Logs are more expensive to index than traces"", ""Logs cannot be sampled"", ""Log pipelines require a queue"""]",0,"Logs dwarf metrics and traces in volume, are expensive to index, and are commonly retained far longer, which makes them the first thing to reduce through sampling, filtering at collection, and moving to cheaper archive storage. Cardinality matters in logs too, since every unique label multiplies stored series.",MEDIUM,cost
monitoring,"What does trace sampling in a distributed system typically cost you?","[""You lose visibility into the errors you did not sample, so error-biased sampling is usually preferable"", ""Nothing, since traces are complete"", ""Increased storage cost"", ""Traces become inaccurate in latency"""]",0,"Head-based sampling keeps a fixed percentage and can easily drop the one trace that explains an intermittent failure. Tail-based sampling buffers and decides per complete trace, which lets you keep all errors and slow traces while discarding boring successes, at the cost of added buffer latency.",HARD,tracing
monitoring,"What is the purpose of a runbook linked from an alert?","[""It tells the on-call engineer what the alert means and what to do, reducing time to mitigate"", ""It documents the service architecture"", ""It is required for compliance"", ""It replaces the alert"""]",0,"A runbook converts a page into action: what broke, how to confirm it, the safe mitigation, and when to escalate. Without one, the responder spends the incident's most expensive minutes searching. Runbooks should be tested, since an untested one is worse than none.",EASY,oncall
monitoring,"What distinguishes a percentile from a mean when describing latency?","[""A percentile describes a tail experienced by some users; a mean can look healthy while the tail is terrible"", ""Percentiles are always larger than means"", ""Means are more accurate"", ""They are the same when data is uniform"""]",0,"A mean can be dominated by the bulk of fast requests and stay flat while a small, very slow group suffers, which is exactly the group that tweets at you. Track p50, p95 and p99 separately, and remember percentiles cannot be averaged across instances, which is why histograms rather than Summaries are used.",MEDIUM,metrics
`;

