FILES["iam"] = `topic,text,options,correct,explanation,difficulty,tags
iam,"What does an explicit Deny in any applicable IAM policy do?","[""Overrides every Allow from identity, resource, SCP and boundary policies"", ""Is overridden by a resource-based policy Allow"", ""Applies only within one account"", ""Applies only to IAM users"""]",0,"Evaluation is deny-first: an explicit Deny anywhere wins, which is exactly what makes permissions boundaries and SCPs reliable guardrails rather than suggestions. It is also why a broad inline Allow cannot re-open access a boundary has closed.",MEDIUM,policies
iam,"What is the difference between a permissions boundary and an SCP?","[""A boundary caps a principal or resource; an SCP caps everything in an account or OU"", ""They are two names for the same thing"", ""A boundary can only deny, an SCP can only allow"", ""A boundary applies to resources, an SCP to users only"""]",0,"A boundary intersects with identity-based policies and is the maximum a principal can do. An SCP applies to every principal in the account or OU and is the maximum for the whole account. A broad allowlist SCP combined with per-team boundaries is a well-regarded design.",MEDIUM,architecture
iam,"What does the external_id in a role trust policy protect against?","[""The confused deputy problem, where a third party could use their own keys to act as you"", ""Credential expiration"", ""Cross-region access"", ""Cost allocation"""]",0,"A provider holding long-lived AWS keys could call AssumeRole on your role and impersonate you. Requiring an external ID that only you and the provider can construct, with a condition on sts:ExternalId, means a leaked provider key alone is not sufficient. This is a well-known interview question with a specific answer.",HARD,roles
iam,"Why are long-lived IAM access keys discouraged in favour of roles with OIDC?","[""Keys leak through code, logs and CI variables and need manual rotation; roles issue short-lived credentials"", ""IAM users cannot use the console"", ""Roles are more expensive"", ""Only one IAM user is allowed per account"""]",0,"Access keys are the most common cause of cloud breaches because they end up in environment variables, committed config and build logs, and remain valid until someone notices. Roles with OIDC or STS issue credentials lasting minutes to an hour with no rotation to manage, and service accounts automate it entirely.",MEDIUM,best-practices
iam,"What does NotAction do in an IAM policy statement?","[""Matches every action except the listed ones, which is powerful as a Deny and risky as an Allow"", ""Same as Action but scoped to resources"", ""Ignores permissions boundaries"", ""Restricts actions to one service"""]",0,"NotAction is the complement of Action, so new services and actions fall inside it automatically. That makes it dangerous with Allow, because you would be allowing everything you failed to anticipate, while making it a strong guardrail with Deny, for example denying everything except a known list, which survives new AWS services.",HARD,policies
iam,"Which IAM element is required, besides having sts:AssumeRole, for a principal to assume a role?","[""The role's trust policy must name that principal"", ""A permissions boundary on the role"", ""The principal must be an IAM user rather than a role"", ""The role must live in the root account"""]",0,"Identity-based permissions say what a principal may do and the trust policy says who may assume the role. Both sides must agree. Conditions on the trust policy, such as on aws:PrincipalTag, aws:SourceVpce or ExternalId, are how you require MFA or restrict to a specific endpoint.",MEDIUM,roles
iam,"What is the effect of an SCP attached to an organizational unit?","[""It applies as a ceiling to every principal in that OU and its child OUs"", ""It applies only to resources in that OU"", ""It overrides all identity policies to allow everything"", ""It applies only to the OU's roles"""]",0,"An OU-level SCP bounds everything inside, including child OUs, and combines with any parent OU's SCP so the most restrictive wins across the chain. That composition is what makes a hierarchy-based guardrail approach workable in a large organisation.",MEDIUM,architecture
iam,"Why is the aws:SourceVpce condition useful on an S3 bucket policy?","[""It restricts access to requests arriving through a specific VPC endpoint, preventing exfiltration from outside the VPC"", ""It speeds up S3 requests"", ""It replaces bucket encryption"", ""It requires the caller to use a public IP"""]",0,"Conditioning on the VPC endpoint that traffic must have come through prevents someone inside the VPC from bypassing it, and keeps the bucket private while still allowing VPC access. Combined with Block Public Access it gives a defensible posture for sensitive data.",HARD,security
iam,"What does a permissions boundary on a role do when the role has no identity-based policy?","[""Nothing, because a boundary only intersects with identity-based policies to further restrict them"", ""It grants the boundary's own permissions"", ""It acts as a resource policy"", ""It makes the role's session longer"""]",0,"A boundary is a ceiling, not a grant. If the identity-based policy allows nothing, the effective permissions are nothing, because permission requires an Allow from the identity side and the boundary not to deny it. This trips people up when they expect a boundary to be a substitute for a policy.",HARD,policies
iam,"Which statement about IAM Access Analyzer is correct?","[""It analyses whether resources are reachable from outside their account, including public and cross-account access"", ""It audits IAM credential usage only"", ""It enforces SCPs"", ""It reports unused permissions"""]",0,"External Access Analyzer answers whether a resource is exposed to the internet or to another account, which is exactly what S3, IAM and KMS policies need checked for. IAM-specific analysis also surfaces unused permissions, but that is a different feature from external access findings.",MEDIUM,governance
iam,"What is the effect of granting s3:* on an object ARN that does not exist?","[""No access is granted, because the resource must match a real object"", ""Full bucket access is granted"", ""The policy is invalid and ignored"", ""Access is granted to future objects"""]",0,"A resource ARN must match the specific object, so granting on a non-existent object grants nothing today and nothing later, because the ARN encodes the key. Granting on a bucket ARN is what applies to all current and future objects, which is the behaviour to reason about carefully when a bucket holds sensitive data.",MEDIUM,policies
iam,"Why is tag-based ABAC useful in a large organisation?","[""Permissions follow tags, so resources can be granted access without listing every ARN"", ""It is faster to evaluate"", ""It replaces SCPs"", ""It prevents resource deletion"""]",0,"ABAC lets a policy condition on aws:ResourceTag or aws:PrincipalTag, so onboarding a new resource to a team is a tagging action rather than a policy edit. The risk is tag sprawl and untagged resources, so a governance control that enforces tagging is usually required alongside it.",HARD,architecture
`;

